docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:663:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01511VMk8oSYSUSAcVdPbuDj","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md","content":"# T71 — Phase 10 — Extension review-panel scaffolding + JSON-RPC plumbing to new tools\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R70\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task lays the foundation for the entire Phase 10 review surface. It registers a new VS Code webview view under the existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` slot in `extension/package.json`, wires a typed JSON-RPC bridge that translates the new memory/event/verification tool responses into UI state, and bootstraps the i18n catalog that every later subview (T72–T76) depends on. Without this scaffolding, the memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, consolidation queue, indexing health, and graph health subviews have no parent panel, no transport, and no string catalog. Spec §10 names the surface explicitly: \"This can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\" This task delivers that panel skeleton.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: memory inbox; proposed promotions; proposed contradictions; stale memory list; memory evidence view; event trace view; retrieval explanation view; usefulness metrics; workspace graph health; indexing health; consolidation queue.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows.\"\n\n> \"Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\" (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties — binds the RPC bridge to the typed contract.)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R70 (Phase 9 metrics + evaluation review verdict) — confirms the metrics surface that the indexing/graph health views consume is stable, and that the new memory/event/verification tools introduced in Phases 6–9 have stable JSON shapes.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/package.json` — `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` is the activity-bar slot the new review view contributes to; the existing `lattice.sidebar` view confirms the pattern.\n  - `extension/src/extension.ts` — current activation function that the new review panel registration plugs into alongside the existing sidebar and docs workbench.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — current JSON-RPC client used to talk to the daemon over stdio; the new RPC bridge wraps this with typed request/response helpers for the review tools.\n  - `extension/src/sidebar.ts`, `extension/src/docsWorkbench.ts` — existing webview patterns to mirror (webview HTML build, message bus, dispose cleanup).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, no inline error swallowing); `ui-specification.md` §3 (accessibility baseline), §7 (modals via native `<dialog>`), §8.1–8.5 (loading, empty, error states), §14 (i18n catalog); `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §10 Human Review Surface and §Phase 10 deliverables in full so the panel scope (eleven operator views) is explicit before writing any code.\n2. Open `extension/package.json` and add a new view contribution under `contributes.views.\"lattice\"` named `lattice.reviewPanel` with `type: \"webview\"`, an explicit title, and an icon path. Keep the existing `lattice.sidebar` view intact so the existing surface continues to function (per `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline).\n3. Add a command contribution `lattice.openReviewPanel` that focuses the new view, and bind it to the activity-bar group so the operator can launch the panel from the existing Lattice container.\n4. Create `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` exporting a `ReviewPanelProvider` class implementing `vscode.WebviewViewProvider`. The provider owns the webview lifecycle (resolve, dispose), sets up CSP headers, and bootstraps the subview router that T72–T76 plug their views into. Set `enableScripts: true`, `localResourceRoots` restricted to the extension's `media/` and `out/` directories.\n5. Create `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` exporting a typed `ReviewRpcBridge` class wrapping the existing `daemon.ts` client. Expose typed methods for the new tools introduced in Phases 6–9 (memory inbox listing, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale memory, evidence inspection, event trace, retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, indexing health, graph health). Every method returns a `Promise<Result>` shape with explicit error variants — never swallow errors (`coding.md` §Error handling).\n6. Each `ReviewRpcBridge` method handler wraps its call in `try/catch` and surfaces failures via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (the VS Code analogue of `parseApiError + toast.error` since `parseApiError` does not apply outside the Cadres web portal). Where a mutation result is also rendered into the webview, the bridge also posts a `{ type: 'error', message }` event to the webview so the subview can render an inline error state.\n7. Create `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` with namespaced keys for every user-facing string the panel scaffolding emits today (panel title, \"loading\", \"no items\", \"error loading data\", retry label, \"unverified\"/\"verified\"/\"in_review\"/\"stale\"/\"contradicted\"/\"superseded\"/\"expired\"/\"invalidated\" status labels, accept/reject action labels). Provide a tiny `i18n.t(key, params?)` helper at `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` that reads from the catalog at module load. ICU placeholders only — never string concatenation (per `ui-specification.md` §14).\n8. Register `ReviewPanelProvider` from `extension/src/extension.ts` during activation, using `vscode.window.registerWebviewViewProvider('lattice.reviewPanel', provider)`. Wire the `lattice.openReviewPanel` command to `vscode.commands.executeCommand('lattice.reviewPanel.focus')`.\n9. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error and warning before stopping. Confirm the new view shows up in the existing Lattice activity bar container without disturbing the existing sidebar.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — `ReviewPanelProvider` (webview lifecycle, subview router, CSP, dispose) using the existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` slot.\n- `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed JSON-RPC bridge wrapping `daemon.ts` for every Phase 6–9 review tool, with `try/catch` + `vscode.window.showErrorMessage` for every mutation.\n- `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` — initial i18n catalog (panel title, status labels, action labels, common loading/empty/error strings) keyed by namespace.\n- `extension/package.json` — extended with `lattice.reviewPanel` webview view contribution and `lattice.openReviewPanel` command, both under the existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` container.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — confirms the provider file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — confirms the RPC bridge file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` — confirms the i18n catalog exists for T72–T76 to consume.\n- `cd extension && npm install` — installs dependencies cleanly without lockfile churn.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (`wc -l extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Webview message-handler dispatch uses a table-driven switch, not nested ifs.\n- Single source of truth: the new RPC bridge is the only path the review subviews use to talk to the daemon; existing `daemon.ts` is wrapped, never duplicated.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every `await` either bubbles via `try/catch` to `vscode.window.showErrorMessage` or returns a typed `Result`.\n- Doc citation: code comments that explain panel scope cite `## 10. Human Review Surface` from the plan doc (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n### UI specification\n\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` (loaded from `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json`); no inline English literals in `reviewPanel.ts`, `rpcBridge.ts`, or the webview HTML this scaffolding emits.\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` (per `ui-specification.md` §7); no `window.confirm`, `window.prompt`, `window.alert`, or any imperative dialog API in the scaffolding or anywhere the subviews mount through it.\n- All mutation handlers use `try/catch`. Because this is a VS Code extension (not the Cadres web portal), the canonical Cadres `parseApiError(e) + toast.error(...)` pair does not apply directly — instead, every catch surfaces the error via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)`, mirroring the same \"never swallow, always surface\" intent from `ui-specification.md` §8.5.\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` where applicable to webview UI. For VS Code tree-view interactions (e.g. picking a workspace, branch, memory class), use `vscode.window.showQuickPick` with typed entries — never raw `<select>` or `prompt`-based ID entry.\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` patterns where applicable. The scaffolding itself does not render a table; T72 onwards do, but the panel must expose hooks/utilities so subviews mount their tables with consistent sort state.\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface. The scaffolding ships baseline component primitives (status-badge stub, loading skeleton stub, empty-state stub, error banner) used by all subviews; every subview built on top must render the appropriate state.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] UI specification checks satisfied: i18n catalog present, no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, every mutation handler surfaces errors via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the panel scaffolding, RPC bridge, i18n catalog, and view registration are all completed in this task; subviews are sibling Phase 10 tasks (T72–T76), not deferrals.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` so the extension picks up the registered view.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"59856aba-c13f-4817-a0b9-f207f5b84930","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:664:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01511VMk8oSYSUSAcVdPbuDj","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2635c0f8-fa4f-45b2-8324-af166f5dd245","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:35:25.881Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:681:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JrQgrD3saDGmKZCe7A7FBY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T72.md","content":"# T72 — Phase 10 — Memory inbox view + filters + status badges\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T71\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§8. Verification Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#8-verification-engine)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the memory inbox — the operator's primary list view over every memory the daemon knows about. It mounts inside the review panel scaffolding from T71, lists memories with filterable columns (status, scope, class), and renders one status badge per row covering all eight verification states from spec §Verification Engine. The inbox is the entry point operators use to find the memory they want to verify, accept, reject, or inspect — without it, the promotion queue (T73), contradiction queue (T73), stale view (T74), and evidence inspector (T74) have nothing to link to. Spec §10 lists \"memory inbox\" first among the required operator views, which fixes its position as the spine of the review surface.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: memory inbox; proposed promotions; proposed contradictions; stale memory list; memory evidence view; event trace view; retrieval explanation view; usefulness metrics; workspace graph health; indexing health; consolidation queue.\" (spec §10)\n\n> \"Verification outputs: `verified`; `unverified`; `in_review`; `stale`; `contradicted`; `superseded`; `expired`; `invalidated`.\" (spec §8 — every status the badge must render)\n\n> \"Each memory record requires: stable id; content; memory class; assertion type; scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization; verification status; confidence and confidence reason; freshness policy; ... last verified state.\" (spec §4 — drives the filter axes)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T71 (review panel scaffolding) — supplies the `ReviewPanelProvider`, the typed `ReviewRpcBridge`, and the i18n catalog this view extends.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — host webview the inbox mounts into via the subview router.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed `listMemories({ status?, scope?, class? })` method this view calls.\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` — i18n catalog this view extends with inbox-specific keys.\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3); `ui-specification.md` §6 (lists & tables with required pagination and sortable headers), §8.2–8.4 (loading/empty/error/success states), §11.1 (status-badge palette), §14 (i18n catalog).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §4 Memory Graph fields and §8 Verification Engine status set so the filter taxonomy and badge palette are grounded in the spec, not invented.\n2. Create `extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts` exporting a `mountMemoryInbox(host, bridge, i18n)` function that the review panel router calls when the operator opens the \"Inbox\" subview. The function returns a `dispose()` handle for clean teardown.\n3. Build the inbox UI inside the webview as a sortable table with columns: `Status` (badge), `Class`, `Scope`, `Content (truncated)`, `Last Verified`, `Confidence`, `Actions` (View Evidence, Open Trace). Render the table via the table primitives exposed by T71's scaffolding; use the `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents for the webview context.\n4. Above the table render a filter bar with three controls: `Status` (multi-select over the eight statuses from §8), `Scope` (multi-select over `session`, `branch`, `repo`, `user`, `organization` from §4), `Class` (multi-select over the memory classes from §4 including `Observation`, `Decision`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, `AntiPattern`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `FailurePattern`, `Procedure`, `Preference`, `ArchitectureInvariant`, `DocsContract`, `OpenQuestion`, `CounterMemory`). Each control is a `vscode.window.showQuickPick` invocation triggered from a button (no raw `<select>`); the chosen values post back to the webview which re-queries the bridge.\n5. Create `extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts` exporting `renderMemoryRow(memory, i18n)`. The function returns the DOM snippet for one row, encapsulating column formatting, truncation (per `ui-specification.md` §12.3), and `data-testid` attributes for telemetry. No business logic — pure rendering.\n6. Create `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts` exporting `renderStatusBadge(status: VerificationStatus, i18n)`. Map each of the eight statuses to a DaisyUI-style semantic color (`badge-success`, `badge-warning`, `badge-error`, etc. — see `ui-specification.md` §11.1) plus an i18n label key. The badge palette is the only place this mapping lives — no inline color logic elsewhere in the review surface.\n7. Add inbox-specific i18n keys to `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` under a `memoryInbox` namespace: column headers, filter labels, empty-state message (\"No memories match the current filters.\"), error-state message, loading skeleton hint, and per-status badge labels (already partly present from T71; reuse keys instead of forking).\n8. Wire the row click handler to post a message back to `ReviewPanelProvider` requesting navigation to the evidence inspector (T74). Wire the \"Open Trace\" action to request navigation to the event trace view (T75).\n9. Wrap every bridge call in `try/catch`. On error, render the inline error banner from T71 and call `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` so the error is visible at both surfaces.\n10. Run `cd extension && npm run compile && npm run lint` and resolve every error/warning before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts` — `mountMemoryInbox(host, bridge, i18n)` subview controller; owns table state, filter state, pagination, and row click routing.\n- `extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts` — pure rendering helper for one memory row with truncation and `data-testid` attributes.\n- `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts` — single-source-of-truth status badge with all eight verification statuses from spec §8 mapped to color tokens and i18n labels.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts` — confirms the inbox controller file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts` — confirms the row component exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (`wc -l extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Filter combinator is a pure function, not a nested branch tree.\n- Single source of truth: `StatusBadge.ts` is the only place verification-status→color/label mapping lives; the promotion queue, stale view, and inspector reuse it.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every bridge call has a `try/catch` and surfaces failure to both the webview banner and the VS Code error toast.\n- Doc citation: comments cite `## 8. Verification Engine` for the status palette and `## 10. Human Review Surface` for the subview scope (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n### UI specification\n\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` from the `memoryInbox` namespace in `en.json`; no inline English literals in any of the three files.\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` — the inbox itself does not open modals (action buttons route to T73/T74 which own their dialogs), but any inline confirm (e.g. \"Clear all filters?\") uses `<dialog>` or routes through `vscode.window.showInformationMessage` with explicit actions. Never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`.\n- All mutation handlers (filter changes are reads, not mutations, but row-action handlers and any future inline mutation) use `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (`parseApiError` does not apply in the VS Code extension context).\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (webview) or `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (tree-view interactions). Filter pickers use `showQuickPick`; no raw `<select>` or `type=\"number\"` for any ID field.\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents exposed by T71's scaffolding; the inbox table sorts on `Status`, `Class`, `Scope`, `Last Verified`, and `Confidence` columns.\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface: status badges via `StatusBadge.ts`; loading skeleton on first load (`ui-specification.md` §8.2); empty state with distinct messages for \"no memories yet\" vs \"no memories match filters\" (`ui-specification.md` §8.3); error banner inline + VS Code error toast on failure (`ui-specification.md` §8.5).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] UI specification checks satisfied: all eight verification statuses render via `StatusBadge.ts`; filters cover status, scope, and class as required by spec §10; i18n coverage complete; loading/empty/error states present.\n- [ ] No deferred work: all filter axes from spec §10 wired in this task; row-click navigation handed off to T74/T75 (sibling tasks), not deferred.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"b946ce15-8224-4c11-a3e6-dfe6d00ed43f","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
