docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:705:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F3DH5Fpg8roxDtQWKpDiPK","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T73.md","content":"# T73 — Phase 10 — Promotion queue + contradiction queue with accept/reject (dialog modals)\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T71\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§6. Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#6-consolidation-engine), [§Opaque Memory Mutation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#opaque-memory-mutation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers two of the most consequential operator surfaces in Phase 10: the promotion queue (proposed promotions awaiting accept/reject) and the contradiction queue (memory pairs the consolidation engine flagged as conflicting, awaiting a human verdict). Per spec §Opaque Memory Mutation, \"automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge\" is a named risk whose control is \"event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\" This task implements the human side of that control: the queue UIs and the proposal-confirm dialogs that turn a proposed memory evolution into an applied write only when an operator explicitly clicks accept. Dialogs are native `<dialog>` elements per the UI specification — never `window.confirm`.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: ... proposed promotions; proposed contradictions; ... one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals.\" (spec §10, §Phase 10 deliverables)\n\n> \"Each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory.\" (spec §6 — binds the accept/reject contract.)\n\n> \"Risk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge. Control: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\" (spec §Opaque Memory Mutation)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T71 (review panel scaffolding) — supplies `ReviewPanelProvider`, `ReviewRpcBridge`, the i18n catalog, and the base modal primitive (native `<dialog>` mounted into the webview).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — host the two queue subviews mount into.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed methods this task consumes: `listPromotionProposals`, `applyPromotion`, `rejectPromotion`, `listContradictions`, `applyContradictionResolution`, `rejectContradictionResolution`.\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts` (from T72) — reused for proposal status badges (`pending`, `applied`, `rejected`, `expired`).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (no `window.confirm`, error handling, single source of truth, schema parity); `ui-specification.md` §7 (modals are native `<dialog>`, never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`), §7.2 (destructive-action confirms), §8.4 (toast feedback on mutations), §8.7 (success message phrasing), §8.5 (mandatory error display), §14 (i18n catalog).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §6 Consolidation Engine, the \"LLM-driven consolidation\" subsection, §10 Human Review Surface, and §Opaque Memory Mutation risk control in full so the proposal-only-then-apply contract is grounded in the spec.\n2. Create `extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts` exporting `mountPromotionQueue(host, bridge, i18n)`. The function lists pending promotion proposals (memory candidates the consolidation engine wants to elevate, e.g. session → branch, branch → repo) with columns: `Proposed Class`, `Current Scope`, `Target Scope`, `Confidence`, `Evidence Count`, `Created At`, `Status` badge, `Actions` (Review, Accept, Reject).\n3. Create `extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts` exporting `mountContradictionQueue(host, bridge, i18n)`. The function lists pending contradiction pairs with columns: `Memory A`, `Memory B`, `Link Type` (`contradicts`/`supersedes` candidate), `Detected By` (rule or LLM proposal provenance), `Created At`, `Status` badge, `Actions` (Review, Accept Resolution, Reject Resolution).\n4. Create `extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts` exporting `openProposalDialog({ proposal, onApply, onReject }, i18n)`. The dialog uses a native `<dialog>` element mounted into the webview (never `window.confirm`). The dialog renders: proposal summary, evidence list with file/symbol/event links, model + prompt-hash + response-hash provenance (per spec §6 LLM-driven consolidation), expected effect on durable memory, and a `Diff preview` panel showing the memory record before/after. Footer: `Cancel`, `Reject (with reason)`, `Accept and apply`.\n5. The dialog enforces typed-confirm for high-scope proposals (per `ui-specification.md` §7.2): if the target scope is `repo`, `user`, or `organization`, the operator must type the memory class name (e.g. `ArchitectureInvariant`) before the Accept button enables. Below `repo` scope, single-click accept is sufficient.\n6. The `onApply` handler calls `bridge.applyPromotion(proposalId)` (or `bridge.applyContradictionResolution(...)` for the contradiction dialog). Wrap in `try/catch`; on success post a `vscode.window.showInformationMessage('Promotion applied.')` and refresh the queue. On failure, render an inline error inside the dialog and call `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (per `ui-specification.md` §8.5).\n7. The `onReject` handler requires a reason string (rendered as a `<textarea>` inside the dialog, required to be non-empty). Calls `bridge.rejectPromotion(proposalId, reason)`. Reasons are persisted by the daemon as event-log evidence so rejected proposals are auditable per spec §Opaque Memory Mutation control.\n8. Add queue-specific i18n keys to `en.json` under `promotionQueue` and `contradictionQueue` namespaces: column headers, action labels, dialog title, dialog body strings, typed-confirm prompt, success/error toast strings. Status-badge labels reused from T71.\n9. Wrap every bridge call in `try/catch`. On read failure render the inline error banner; on mutation failure render inline + `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)`.\n10. Run `cd extension && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error/warning before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts` — `mountPromotionQueue` subview controller listing pending promotions and routing actions through `ProposalDialog`.\n- `extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts` — `mountContradictionQueue` subview controller listing pending contradictions and routing actions through `ProposalDialog`.\n- `extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts` — single shared native `<dialog>` modal used by both queues, with typed-confirm gating for high-scope proposals.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts` — confirms the promotion queue file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts` — confirms the contradiction queue file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts` — confirms the dialog component exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Dialog state machine is a discriminated union, not nested booleans.\n- Single source of truth: `ProposalDialog.ts` is the only modal used by both queues; promotion and contradiction queues parameterize it via props rather than forking.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every bridge call surfaces failure to the dialog and to `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n- Doc citation: comments cite `## 6. Consolidation Engine` for the proposal-only-then-apply contract and `## Opaque Memory Mutation` for the audit-trail requirement (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n### UI specification\n\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` from the `promotionQueue`, `contradictionQueue`, and `proposalDialog` namespaces; no inline literals in any of the three files.\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` (per `ui-specification.md` §7) — `ProposalDialog.ts` mounts an actual `<dialog>` element in the webview and uses `dialog.showModal()` / `dialog.close()`. No `window.confirm`, no `window.prompt`, no `window.alert`, no DaisyUI `modal modal-open` `<div>` pattern. Typed-confirm for high-scope (repo / user / organization) per `ui-specification.md` §7.2.\n- All mutation handlers (`onApply`, `onReject`) use `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (`parseApiError + toast.error` is the Cadres web equivalent; for the VS Code extension this is the analogue per the build harness rule).\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (webview) or `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (tree-view) — proposal selection inside the dialog uses `<dialog>` content, not a separate ID picker; if cross-references to other memories or events are picked, use `showQuickPick`, never raw `<select>` or `prompt`.\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents on both queue tables. Sortable on `Confidence`, `Evidence Count`, `Created At`, and `Status`.\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface: badge for proposal status, loading skeleton on first load, distinct empty states (\"No pending promotions.\" vs \"No pending contradictions.\"), error banner on read failure, inline dialog error on apply/reject failure plus VS Code error toast.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] UI specification checks satisfied: native `<dialog>`, no `window.confirm`, typed-confirm for high-scope proposals, error handling on every mutation, reason required on every reject.\n- [ ] No deferred work: both queues + the shared dialog ship in this task; stale view (T74), event trace (T75), and graph/index health (T76) are sibling tasks, not deferrals.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"fcc99437-4fc6-4d24-96fb-320f9dce7740","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:722:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015RZijFYrtWDbQk3WHP7xt8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T74.md","content":"# T74 — Phase 10 — Stale memory view + evidence inspector + verification trigger\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T71\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§8. Verification Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#8-verification-engine), [§Stale Memory Leakage](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#stale-memory-leakage)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the stale-memory view and the evidence inspector, plus the on-demand verification trigger that calls the Phase 8 `verify_memory` tool. The stale view is the operator's lever to see every memory whose linked files, symbols, docs, or tests have drifted; the evidence inspector is the deep-dive surface for any single memory (used both from the inbox in T72 and the stale view here); and the verification trigger lets an operator re-run verification on demand instead of waiting for the background verifier. Together they implement the human-side control for spec §Stale Memory Leakage: \"agents trust outdated memory; control: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\"\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: ... stale memory list; memory evidence view; ...\" (spec §10)\n\n> \"Verification checks: linked files still exist; linked symbols still exist; cited docs still exist; linked tests still exist; evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured; implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible; contradicted/superseded states remain coherent; branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches; time-bound memory has expired.\" (spec §8)\n\n> \"Verification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\" (spec §8 — binds the on-demand trigger to target single memories, not full sweeps.)\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: ... `verify_memory`; ...\" (spec §9)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T71 (review panel scaffolding + RPC bridge + i18n catalog + base modal primitive).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — host the two subviews mount into.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed methods this task consumes: `listStaleMemories({ scope?, class? })`, `getMemoryEvidence(memoryId)`, `verifyMemory(memoryId)` (the Phase 8 `verify_memory` tool).\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts` (from T72) — reused for stale/verified/in_review badge rendering.\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md`; `ui-specification.md` §6 (lists & tables), §8.2–8.5 (loading/empty/error), §11.1 (status badges), §14 (i18n).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §8 Verification Engine and §Stale Memory Leakage in full so the verification status taxonomy and the \"stale label in all surfaces\" requirement are grounded in the spec.\n2. Create `extension/src/review/staleView.ts` exporting `mountStaleView(host, bridge, i18n)`. List every memory whose verification status is `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, or `invalidated` (per spec §8). Columns: `Status` badge, `Class`, `Scope`, `Content (truncated)`, `Stale Since`, `Stale Reason` (e.g. \"linked symbol removed\", \"evidence span no longer matches\", \"branch scope drift\"), `Actions` (Inspect Evidence, Re-verify).\n3. Add filter controls for `Status` (multi-select over the five stale-family statuses) and `Scope` (multi-select). Filters route through `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (no raw `<select>`).\n4. Create `extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts` exporting `mountEvidenceInspector(host, bridge, i18n, memoryId)`. The inspector is the deep-dive surface for one memory, mounted as a full-panel subview (not a modal — it carries too much content for a `<dialog>`). It renders:\n   - Memory header: id, class, scope, status badge, confidence + reason.\n   - Content panel: the memory's textual claim.\n   - Evidence list: every `memory_evidence` row linked to this memory, with file/symbol/doc/test references that resolve to clickable `vscode.commands.executeCommand('vscode.open', uri)` links.\n   - Provenance list: every event from the event log that touched this memory (created, updated, verified, invalidated, consolidated), with timestamp, actor, and link to the event trace view (T75).\n   - Link list: every `memory_links` row (supports, contradicts, supersedes, refines, etc.) with link type, target, strength, and reason.\n   - Verification panel: last verified at, last verification result, and a `Re-verify now` button.\n5. Wire the `Re-verify now` button to call `bridge.verifyMemory(memoryId)`. Show a loading spinner on the button while the call is in flight; on success refresh the inspector and surface a `vscode.window.showInformationMessage('Verification complete: <new status>.')`; on failure render an inline error banner and call `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)`.\n6. Stale view rows also expose a `Re-verify` action that calls `bridge.verifyMemory(memoryId)` without opening the inspector. Pattern is the same — loading state on the row, success refresh, failure surfaces both inline and via VS Code error toast.\n7. Add i18n keys under `staleView` and `evidenceInspector` namespaces: column headers, action labels, stale-reason translation table (one key per documented reason from spec §8 verification checks), button labels, success/error strings, loading hint, empty state.\n8. Cross-link: stale view row click navigates to the evidence inspector; evidence inspector \"Open Trace\" button navigates to the event trace view (T75). Use the subview router from T71's `ReviewPanelProvider`.\n9. Wrap every bridge call in `try/catch` and surface failure both inline and via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n10. Run `cd extension && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error/warning before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/review/staleView.ts` — `mountStaleView` subview controller listing stale-family memories with filters and per-row re-verify action.\n- `extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts` — `mountEvidenceInspector` deep-dive subview for one memory; renders evidence, provenance, links, and an on-demand verification trigger calling `verify_memory` per spec §9.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/review/staleView.ts` — confirms the stale view file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts` — confirms the evidence inspector file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Evidence renderer is a table-driven function over evidence types, not a nested switch.\n- Single source of truth: status badge from T72; stale-reason translation table is the only place verification-check labels live in the UI.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every bridge call surfaces failure inline + via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n- Doc citation: comments cite `## 8. Verification Engine` for the status taxonomy and `## Stale Memory Leakage` for the surfacing requirement (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n### UI specification\n\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` from the `staleView` and `evidenceInspector` namespaces; no inline literals in either file.\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` — the evidence inspector is a panel, not a modal; if an inline confirm is needed (e.g. for a destructive re-verify on a high-scope memory), use `<dialog>` or `vscode.window.showInformationMessage` with explicit actions. Never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`.\n- All mutation handlers (the re-verify trigger) use `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (`parseApiError + toast.error` is the Cadres web equivalent; for VS Code, the analogue is the error toast).\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (webview) or `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (tree-view) — stale view filters use `showQuickPick`; cross-memory navigation from the inspector uses `showQuickPick` when picking from many. Never raw `<select>` or `prompt`-based ID entry.\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents on the stale view: sortable on `Status`, `Class`, `Scope`, `Stale Since`.\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface: status badges via `StatusBadge.ts`; loading skeleton on first load and per-row spinner during re-verify; distinct empty states (\"No stale memories. Verification is up to date.\" vs \"No stale memories match the current filters.\"); error banner inline + VS Code toast on failure.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] UI specification checks satisfied: all five stale-family statuses render via `StatusBadge.ts`; re-verify trigger present on both surfaces; cross-navigation to event trace (T75) wired through router.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the stale view, evidence inspector, and on-demand `verify_memory` trigger all ship in this task; event trace (T75) and health views (T76) are sibling tasks, not deferrals.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"58ffeab5-c9ff-4cab-a20b-43434a487b81","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:737:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X7TBMs5StdT9wTBVrcLRjf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T75.md","content":"# T75 — Phase 10 — Event trace view + retrieval explanation view\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T71\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log), [§7. Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers two diagnostic surfaces: the event trace view (operator-facing renderer for the append-only event log filtered by session/task/workspace) and the retrieval explanation view (renders the ranking scores + reasons emitted by the Phase 4 retrieval diagnostic mode). They are the surfaces operators use to answer two binding questions: \"what actually happened in this session?\" (event trace) and \"why did this context get included and that other thing get excluded?\" (retrieval explanation). Spec §10 lists both as required; spec §7 requires the ranker to expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode, which is the data shape this UI consumes.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: ... event trace view; retrieval explanation view; ...\" (spec §10)\n\n> \"The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\" (spec §7)\n\n> \"Events should carry: workspace id; branch; session id; task id; actor; timestamp; stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories; payload hash; compact payload summary; optional full payload location.\" (spec §3 — every column the event trace view renders)\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: ... `get_event_trace`.\" (spec §9)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T71 (review panel scaffolding + RPC bridge + i18n catalog).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — host the two subviews mount into.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed methods this task consumes: `getEventTrace({ sessionId?, taskId?, workspaceId?, since?, until? })`, `getRetrievalExplanation({ requestId })` (the Phase 4 diagnostic-mode payload).\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts` (from T72) — reused for event-kind badges and retrieval-decision badges (`included`, `excluded`, `expanded`).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md`; `ui-specification.md` §6 (lists & tables; required pagination on every list per §6.1), §8.2–8.5 (loading/empty/error), §11.1 (status-style badges), §14 (i18n).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §3 Event Log (event types and envelope fields) and §7 Retrieval Engine (pipeline steps, candidate sources, ranking signals, diagnostic mode requirement) so the column sets are grounded in the spec, not invented.\n2. Create `extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts` exporting `mountEventTraceView(host, bridge, i18n, { sessionId?, taskId?, memoryId? })`. The optional initial filter lets the inbox (T72) and evidence inspector (T74) deep-link directly to the relevant trace slice. Render a sortable, paginated table with columns: `Timestamp`, `Kind` badge, `Actor`, `Task`, `Session`, `Workspace`, `References` (compact summary of `StableRef`s), `Summary` (compact payload summary), `Payload` (expand to inline view via `<dialog>`).\n3. Filter bar: `Kind` (multi-select over the 20 event kinds from spec §3), `Actor` (multi-select over `Assistant`, `User`, `Tool`, `Daemon`), `Session`, `Task`, `Workspace`, `Branch`. Filters route through `vscode.window.showQuickPick`. Time window is two text inputs validated against ISO 8601.\n4. Payload expansion uses a native `<dialog>` that renders the structured payload as a syntax-highlighted JSON tree (collapsible) with `Copy to clipboard` and `Open in editor` actions. Never `window.confirm`/`prompt`/`alert`.\n5. Create `extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts` exporting `mountRetrievalExplanationView(host, bridge, i18n, requestId)`. The subview is a single-request deep dive that renders:\n   - Request header: tool name, task statement, intent classification (from Phase 4 T27), timestamp.\n   - Anchors panel: every resolved anchor (file/symbol/error/command/API/config-key per spec §7) with its resolution provenance.\n   - Candidate table: every candidate retrieved across the seven candidate sources from spec §7, with columns `Source`, `Identity`, `Inclusion Decision` badge (`included`/`excluded`/`expanded`), `Score`, `Top Signals` (truncated list of the top three ranking signals from spec §7), `Reason` (compact explanation).\n   - Excluded-candidate panel: high-scoring candidates that were intentionally excluded, with the exclusion reason — spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles requires \"excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\" to be recorded.\n   - Full-signal panel: per-candidate expandable view listing every ranking signal from spec §7 (task-type compatibility, graph proximity, exact identifier match, semantic similarity, verification status, freshness, scope, evidence strength, contradiction/supersession state, past usefulness, recent successful reuse, user-preference compatibility, token cost) with the candidate's score on each.\n6. Wire cross-navigation: from the event trace, clicking a `MemoryRetrieved` event opens the retrieval explanation view for that request id; clicking a memory reference opens the evidence inspector (T74); clicking a file/symbol reference opens the file in VS Code via `vscode.commands.executeCommand('vscode.open', uri)`.\n7. Add i18n keys under `eventTraceView` and `retrievalExplanationView` namespaces: column headers, filter labels, all 20 event-kind labels, all 13 ranking-signal labels, decision badge labels, dialog title, copy/open action labels, success/error strings, loading hint, empty state.\n8. Wrap every bridge call in `try/catch` and surface failure both inline and via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n9. Run `cd extension && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error/warning before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts` — `mountEventTraceView` subview controller; sortable + paginated event-log viewer with kind/actor/session/task filters and payload expansion via native `<dialog>`.\n- `extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts` — `mountRetrievalExplanationView` subview controller; renders the Phase 4 diagnostic payload (anchors, candidates, decisions, exclusions, full signal table).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts` — confirms the event trace file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts` — confirms the retrieval explanation file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. If the retrieval explanation grows beyond, split per panel (`anchors.ts`, `candidates.ts`, `signals.ts`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Signal-table renderer is table-driven over the 13 signal types, not nested ifs.\n- Single source of truth: event-kind labels and ranking-signal labels each live in exactly one i18n namespace; reused across both subviews.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every bridge call surfaces failure inline + via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n- Doc citation: comments cite `## 3. Event Log` for the envelope fields and `## 7. Retrieval Engine` for the diagnostic-mode contract (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n### UI specification\n\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` from the `eventTraceView` and `retrievalExplanationView` namespaces; no inline literals in either file.\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` — payload expansion in the event trace uses `<dialog>`. Never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`.\n- All mutation handlers — these views are predominantly read-only; the `Copy to clipboard` and `Open in editor` actions on the payload dialog use `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` on failure (`parseApiError` does not apply in the VS Code extension context).\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (webview) or `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (tree-view) — session/task/workspace/branch filter pickers all use `showQuickPick`. Never raw `<select>` or `prompt`-based ID entry.\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents on the event trace (sortable on `Timestamp`, `Kind`, `Actor`) and on the candidate table (sortable on `Score`, `Source`, `Decision`). Pagination is required on every list per `ui-specification.md` §6.1.\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface: event-kind badges + decision badges via the shared component; loading skeleton on first load; distinct empty states (\"No events for the selected filters.\" vs \"No retrieval request found for this id.\"); error banner inline + VS Code toast on failure.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] UI specification checks satisfied: all 20 event kinds rendered; all 13 ranking signals rendered; payload expansion via native `<dialog>`; pagination on every list.\n- [ ] No deferred work: both subviews + the cross-navigation wiring ship in this task; the health views (T76) and smoke tests (T77) are sibling tasks, not deferrals.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2f37cd48-04bf-48f6-98a6-c0f1c5dad34e","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:743:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0139kC52VEu3YD1r5ACQ9dAL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016gvQUZeZmcnYPT16NbkckX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T61.md","content":"# T61 — Phase 8 — Tool surface audit + final MCP reference doc (collapse overlapping tools per spec §MCP discipline)\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** docs\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T56, T57, T58, T59, T60\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task produces the audited final MCP tool surface for the cognitive workspace fork: an audit doc recording which tools were collapsed and why, and the canonical reference doc enumerating every tool the daemon ships, its render modes, expansion handles, budget controls, deprecation status, and shim policy. Spec §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\" makes this audit binding: \"ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients … The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" Without this task, the Phase 8 work ships an unaudited surface and Phase 9 metrics regress against the wrong set of tool names.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Every tool response should support: compact rendering, full structured JSON, context handles, stable expansion targets, budget controls, diagnostic explanations where useful.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Required workflow tools: `get_context_capsule`, `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `trace_scenario`, `diagnose_failure`, `find_relevant_tests`, `impact_from_diff`, `get_docs_capsule`, `get_backlinks`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `get_repo_playbook`, `search_symbols`, `search_memory`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T56 (`workflow_v2/` edit-planning tools) — final names and request/response schemas.\n  - T57 (`workflow_v2/` discovery tools) — final names and request/response schemas.\n  - T58 (`memory_v2/` get_task_memory + save_memory + unified `propose_memory_evolution`).\n  - T59 (`memory_v2/` unified `verify_explain_memory` + `list_memory_conflicts`).\n  - T60 (`memory_v2/` consolidate_session + get_memory_metrics + get_event_trace).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (from T02) — must be referenced for deprecation policy and shim removal cadence.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — current registered tool list (43 today); the audit doc must reconcile every entry against the final list.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Single source of truth, §No broken windows; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references — the reference doc is the authoritative MCP tool listing and must be cited by every subsequent doc.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface in full and quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim in `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` as the framing for the audit.\n2. In `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`, write a `## Audit findings` section that lists every collapse decision made during Phase 8: (a) `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` → unified `propose_memory_evolution` with `action` parameter (per T58); (b) `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` → unified `verify_explain_memory` with `mode` parameter (per T59); plus any further collapses identified while reviewing T56–T60. For each collapse, record: rationale, the spec sentence authorizing it, the deprecation shim policy, and the shim-removal deadline.\n3. In `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`, write a `## Collapsed tools` section that is a table or bullet list mapping each spec-nominal tool name to its actual implementation: `apply_memory_evolution → propose_memory_evolution(action=\"apply\")`; `verify_memory → verify_explain_memory(mode=\"verify\")`; `explain_memory → verify_explain_memory(mode=\"explain\")`; and any others identified. Each row links to the implementing file under `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/` or `workflow_v2/`.\n4. In `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`, write a `## Decisions not to collapse` section documenting any spec-nominal pair that was considered but NOT collapsed (e.g. `get_context_capsule` vs `get_docs_capsule` remain separate because they anchor on distinct identity types), with rationale.\n5. In `2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md`, write a `## Final tool list` section enumerating every tool the daemon ships after Phase 8. For each tool, include: canonical name, one-paragraph description, request schema summary, response schema summary, supported render modes (`compact` / `full` / `diagnostic` / handles), budget controls, emitted Phase 2 events, deprecation status, implementing file path. The list must include every spec §MCP Surface \"Required workflow tools\" and every new memory tool (counting the collapsed names) plus the deprecation shims kept active per the T02 compatibility policy.\n6. In `2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md`, add sections `## Render modes`, `## Expansion handles`, `## Budget controls`, `## Deprecation policy`, each citing the corresponding spec heading.\n7. Cross-link `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` ↔ `2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` and reference back to spec §MCP Surface, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, and the T02 compatibility policy by exact heading.\n8. Confirm no spec §MCP Surface \"Required workflow tools\" entry is missing from the reference. Confirm no implemented tool is missing from the reference. The reference doc is single source of truth.\n9. Confirm both docs respect the Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits` (file ≤800 lines); split into two docs is already mandated by the verification commands.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` — canonical reference enumerating every shipped MCP tool, render modes, expansion handles, budget controls, deprecation status, and implementing file paths.\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — audit findings recording every tool collapse decision per spec §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline,\" including the table of collapsed tools and the deprecation shim policy.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` — confirms the reference doc exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — confirms the audit doc exists at the harness path.\n- `grep -q '## Final tool list' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` — confirms the reference enumerates the final tool list under a dedicated heading rather than burying it.\n- `grep -q '## Audit findings' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — confirms the audit findings section exists under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Collapsed tools' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — confirms the collapsed-tools mapping is recorded under a dedicated heading rather than scattered through prose.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (verify with `wc -l docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — not applicable (docs task).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming — applies to any tool/schema names mentioned in the docs; they must match the implementations in T56–T60.\n- For schema changes: the reference doc is the single source of truth; every divergence between docs and code is a contract gate failure caught by R64.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## MCP Surface`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`, and `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2` headings from the spec; reference `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` from T02 for deprecation policy.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every collapse decision is recorded; every shipped tool is listed; no \"to be added later\" placeholders for tool entries.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the T02 compatibility policy was cross-referenced.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4085,"cache_read_input_tokens":77188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4085,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1b19303a-2693-4a8c-9024-c6b34eb8d8d5","request_id":"req_011Cb7N83CrnmzCR4uLXc6tv","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:752:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012qH4jVMW4V9DewbbrQE7KJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T76.md","content":"# T76 — Phase 10 — Consolidation queue + indexing health + workspace graph health views\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T71\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§6. Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#6-consolidation-engine), [§2. Workspace Graph](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#2-workspace-graph)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the three operations-focused subviews of the review panel: the consolidation queue (background and manual-review consolidation jobs with depth/state visibility), the indexing health view (ingestion pipeline state, parser failures, vector index freshness), and the workspace graph health view (node/edge counts per family, broken-link detection, stale-edge detection). These views are how operators answer \"is the daemon healthy and is its substrate trustworthy?\" — without them, only the daemon-side logs show whether consolidation is keeping up, whether parsing is failing silently, or whether the graph has degenerated. Spec §10 names all three explicitly; this task delivers the last three of the eleven required operator views, completing the Phase 10 view set before smoke tests (T77).\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: ... usefulness metrics; workspace graph health; indexing health; consolidation queue.\" (spec §10)\n\n> \"Consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon.\" (spec §6 — drives the queue-depth indicator and dropped-job count.)\n\n> \"The graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\" (spec §2 — drives the freshness/staleness counters in the graph health view.)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T71 (review panel scaffolding + RPC bridge + i18n catalog).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` — host the three subviews mount into.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed methods this task consumes: `listConsolidationJobs({ status?, kind? })`, `getConsolidationQueueDepth()`, `getIndexingHealth()`, `getWorkspaceGraphHealth()`.\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts` (from T72) — reused for job-status badges (`queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `dropped`).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md`; `ui-specification.md` §6 (lists & tables, pagination), §8.2–8.5 (loading/empty/error), §11.1 (status badges), §14 (i18n).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §6 Consolidation Engine (job types, modes, LLM-driven constraints, queue depth rule) and §2 Workspace Graph (node families, edge families, freshness queries) so the column sets are grounded in the spec.\n2. Create `extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts` exporting `mountConsolidationQueueView(host, bridge, i18n)`. Render a sortable, paginated table with columns: `Status` badge, `Kind` (episode summary, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction, duplicate detection, supersession proposal, etc. per spec §6), `Mode` (synchronous / background / manual-review / replay per spec §6 consolidation modes), `Created At`, `Started At`, `Completed At`, `Duration`, `LLM Model` (where applicable), `Result Summary`, `Actions` (Inspect, Retry).\n3. Above the table render a queue-depth indicator showing current depth vs the documented bound, plus a count of jobs `dropped` due to queue full (per spec §6 \"queue depth must be bounded\"). When the dropped count is non-zero, render a warning banner with the count and a link to the event trace view (T75) filtered to `MemoryConsolidated` and consolidation-failure events.\n4. Filter bar: `Status` (multi-select), `Kind` (multi-select), `Mode` (multi-select), all via `vscode.window.showQuickPick`.\n5. Create `extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts` exporting `mountIndexingHealthView(host, bridge, i18n)`. Render a panel layout with sections:\n   - Ingestion pipeline status: per-language parser status (count of files parsed, count failed, last error), watcher status, last full-rescan timestamp.\n   - Vector index health: total embeddings, last rebuild timestamp, average freshness, count of stale embeddings.\n   - FTS index health: row count per FTS table, last rebuild, count of orphan rows.\n   - Event log health: events ingested last hour / 24h / 7d, last compaction snapshot timestamp (per spec §Storage Design event-log compaction), event-payload-spillover row count.\n   - Each section ends with a refresh button that calls the relevant bridge method.\n6. Create `extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts` exporting `mountWorkspaceGraphHealthView(host, bridge, i18n)`. Render per-node-family counts and per-edge-family counts (all node and edge families from spec §2) in tables, plus three diagnostic panels: `Broken references` (edges pointing to non-existent identities), `Stale edges` (edges whose `stale_against` flag is set per spec §2 edge families), and `Orphan symbols` (symbols not referenced by any file). Each panel has a `Recompute` button that triggers a re-verification.\n7. Cross-link: clicking a failed consolidation job opens the event trace view (T75) filtered to the related task/session; clicking a broken-reference row opens the file or symbol via `vscode.commands.executeCommand('vscode.open', uri)`; clicking a stale-edge row opens the evidence inspector (T74) for the related memory if one exists.\n8. Add i18n keys under `consolidationQueueView`, `indexingHealthView`, and `workspaceGraphHealthView` namespaces: every column header, status/kind/mode label, panel title, action label, success/error string, loading hint, empty state.\n9. Wrap every bridge call in `try/catch` and surface failure both inline and via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n10. Run `cd extension && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error/warning before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts` — `mountConsolidationQueueView` subview with sortable/paginated job table, queue-depth indicator, and dropped-job warning per spec §6.\n- `extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts` — `mountIndexingHealthView` panel covering parser, watcher, vector, FTS, and event-log indexing health.\n- `extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts` — `mountWorkspaceGraphHealthView` panel covering per-family counts and three diagnostic panels (broken refs, stale edges, orphan symbols).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts` — confirms the consolidation queue file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts` — confirms the indexing health file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts` — confirms the graph health file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. If a view grows beyond, split per panel.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Panel renderers are table-driven over the documented node/edge/job-kind sets.\n- Single source of truth: job-status badge from T72; node/edge family enumerations live in one place referenced by both the daemon contract and the i18n catalog.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every bridge call surfaces failure inline + via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`.\n- Doc citation: comments cite `## 6. Consolidation Engine` for the queue-depth and dropped-job rule and `## 2. Workspace Graph` for the node/edge family set (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n### UI specification\n\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` from the three namespaces; no inline literals in any of the three files.\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` — these views are mostly panels; the \"Retry job\" confirmation uses a native `<dialog>` or routes through `vscode.window.showInformationMessage` with explicit actions. Never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`.\n- All mutation handlers (Retry, Recompute, refresh) use `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (`parseApiError + toast.error` is the Cadres web equivalent; for VS Code, the analogue is the error toast).\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (webview) or `vscode.window.showQuickPick` (tree-view) — every filter picker uses `showQuickPick`. Never raw `<select>` or `prompt`-based ID entry.\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents on the consolidation queue (sortable on `Status`, `Kind`, `Mode`, `Created At`, `Duration`), per-family counts in graph health (sortable on `Family`, `Count`), and broken-refs / stale-edges / orphan-symbols panels (sortable on `Identity`, `Reason`). Pagination required on every list per `ui-specification.md` §6.1.\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface: badges for every job status; loading skeleton on first load and per-section spinner on refresh; distinct empty states for healthy (\"No queued consolidation jobs.\", \"Indexing pipeline is up to date.\", \"No broken references.\") vs filtered (\"No jobs match the current filters.\"); warning banner when queue-depth bound is hit; error banner inline + VS Code toast on failure.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] UI specification checks satisfied: queue-depth indicator with bound + dropped-job warning per spec §6; per-family counts for all spec §2 node/edge families; cross-navigation to T74/T75 wired through router.\n- [ ] No deferred work: all three views ship in this task; the smoke tests (T77) are a sibling task, not a deferral.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"ce79354a-531c-4b01-8c01-6c9da95c863f","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:767:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VnVHrBqUuRyoA3FBgpwZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LASHxdn2rDrzNDCixBNPTb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T77.md","content":"# T77 — Phase 10 — Extension UI smoke tests (compile + lint + integration runner)\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Testing Requirements](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#testing-requirements)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the VS Code integration smoke tests that prove the Phase 10 review surface activates cleanly inside a real VS Code instance, the review panel registers, and each of the six subviews (memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, evidence inspector, event trace, retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, indexing health, workspace graph health) mounts and renders without throwing. Spec §Testing Requirements lists \"extension compile/UI smoke tests\" as a required test family; this task makes that test family green for the Phase 10 deliverables. Without it, regressions in webview wiring would land unnoticed because compile + lint cannot catch runtime mount failures.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required test families: ... extension compile/UI smoke tests.\" (spec §Testing Requirements)\n\n> \"Definition of done: operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows.\" (spec §Phase 10 — the smoke tests must confirm every subview the operator depends on actually renders.)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T71 (panel scaffolding), T72 (memory inbox), T73 (promotion + contradiction queues), T74 (stale + evidence views), T75 (event trace + retrieval explanation), T76 (consolidation + indexing + graph health) — every subview the smoke tests assert renders.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/package.json` — existing `npm test` script wired to `node ./out/test/runTest.js` (the VS Code test runner pattern documented in `context.md` §\"Tests\").\n  - `extension/src/extension.ts` — activation function under test.\n  - `extension/src/test/` — existing test scaffolding directory (extended here, not replaced).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (tests as documentation §Tests, one test per behavior, integration tests prefer real collaborators); `ui-specification.md` §16 (`data-testid` on every interactive element — tests assert against these).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `extension/package.json` to confirm the test script `npm test` maps to `node ./out/test/runTest.js`. If the runner entry point does not exist, this task is responsible for creating it.\n2. Create `extension/src/test/runTest.ts` exporting the standard VS Code `@vscode/test-electron` runner entry. It downloads VS Code if needed, launches it with the extension under test loaded, runs the Mocha suite from `extension/src/test/suite/index.ts` (or equivalent index file), and exits with the suite's pass/fail code.\n3. If `extension/src/test/suite/index.ts` does not yet exist (check `extension/src/test/`), add a minimal Mocha entry that registers `review.test.ts` and any pre-existing tests so this task does not regress prior coverage.\n4. Create `extension/src/test/review.test.ts` covering:\n   - **Activation**: `vscode.extensions.getExtension('lattice.lattice')!.activate()` returns successfully; `vscode.commands.getCommands(true)` includes `lattice.openReviewPanel`.\n   - **View registration**: After activation, `vscode.window.registerWebviewViewProvider` has been called for `lattice.reviewPanel` (assert by attempting `vscode.commands.executeCommand('lattice.reviewPanel.focus')` and observing no error).\n   - **Subview render: memory inbox**: Open the review panel, route to the inbox subview, assert the webview HTML contains the inbox container element (queried by `data-testid=\"review-memory-inbox\"` per `ui-specification.md` §16) without throwing.\n   - **Subview render: promotion queue**: Route to the promotion queue, assert `data-testid=\"review-promotion-queue\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: contradiction queue**: Assert `data-testid=\"review-contradiction-queue\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: stale view**: Assert `data-testid=\"review-stale-view\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: evidence inspector**: Open with a mock memory id, assert `data-testid=\"review-evidence-inspector\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: event trace**: Assert `data-testid=\"review-event-trace\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: retrieval explanation**: Open with a mock request id, assert `data-testid=\"review-retrieval-explanation\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: consolidation queue**: Assert `data-testid=\"review-consolidation-queue\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: indexing health**: Assert `data-testid=\"review-indexing-health\"` is present.\n   - **Subview render: workspace graph health**: Assert `data-testid=\"review-workspace-graph-health\"` is present.\n   - **No throw on missing data**: For every subview, with the RPC bridge stubbed to return an empty array, assert the subview mounts and renders the documented empty-state string (per `ui-specification.md` §8.3) without throwing.\n   - **No throw on bridge error**: For every subview, with the RPC bridge stubbed to reject, assert the subview mounts and renders the error banner without throwing, and that `vscode.window.showErrorMessage` was called (mock or spy).\n5. Use a thin stub of `ReviewRpcBridge` injected via a test-only export from `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` (add a `setBridgeForTests` hook if not already present from T71). Do not mock VS Code's API — use the real `@vscode/test-electron` runner.\n6. Run the suite locally with `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm test`. Resolve every failure before stopping. Capture the suite output and confirm every named test above runs and passes.\n7. Confirm `cd extension && npm run lint` passes with zero new warnings introduced by the test files (lint must include the `src/test` directory).\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `extension/src/test/review.test.ts` — Mocha test file with the smoke coverage enumerated above: activation, view registration, every subview renders, every subview renders cleanly under empty data, every subview renders cleanly under bridge error.\n- `extension/src/test/runTest.ts` — `@vscode/test-electron` runner entry that `npm test` invokes per `extension/package.json`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f extension/src/test/review.test.ts` — confirms the smoke test file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f extension/src/test/runTest.ts` — confirms the runner entry exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile succeeds with no errors.\n- `cd extension && npm run lint` — lint passes with zero warnings introduced by this task.\n- `cd extension && npm test` — the VS Code test runner launches, the activation test passes, the view-registration test passes, every subview-render test passes, every empty-data test passes, and every bridge-error test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines (each `it(...)` block ≤30 lines per `coding.md` §Hard limits \"Test length\"). Setup factored into beforeEach helpers.\n- One test per behavior, named for the behavior (e.g. `it('mounts the memory inbox without throwing')`), per `coding.md` §Tests as documentation.\n- Integration tests prefer real collaborators — use the real `@vscode/test-electron` runner; mock only the RPC bridge (the process boundary to the daemon).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `// eslint-disable`.\n- No silent error handling — every `await` either propagates to Mocha's failure path or is wrapped in `try/catch` with an explicit assertion.\n- Doc citation: test comments cite `## Testing Requirements` for the smoke-test family requirement and `## 10. Human Review Surface` for the subview catalog (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] Every subview from T72–T76 has a corresponding \"renders without throwing\" test; every subview has an empty-data test; every subview has a bridge-error test.\n- [ ] `data-testid` attributes referenced by tests exist in the rendered HTML — if they were missing, this task adds them in the same change (no broken windows).\n- [ ] No deferred work: the smoke tests cover every subview registered by T71–T76; deeper interaction tests for accept/reject flows are covered by R79 (contract gate), not deferred to a later session.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6111,"cache_read_input_tokens":77427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6111,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"cde47ea5-ed6d-45dc-af76-028da4a4612e","request_id":"req_011Cb7MqWKneiRTEnKhFBPcy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:782:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TwdU3puoByR54shmvCc2Fc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R78.md","content":"# R78 — Frontend review — Phase 10 human review UI (UI-spec compliance)\n\n**Phase:** Review for Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); definition-of-done checklist (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review certifies that the Phase 10 human review UI ships compliant with the shared UI specification, the Lattice coding standard, the spec's mandatory operator view set, and the i18n discipline that all user-facing strings live in a single catalog. It is the gate that R79 (contract gate) and R88 (definition-of-done gate) depend on: a frontend that bypasses `<dialog>`, swallows errors, or inlines English literals would force later phases into rework. Passing this review means the review surface is enterprise-grade on day one per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` (i18n helper)\n  - `extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/staleView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/review.test.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/runTest.ts`\n  - `extension/package.json` (review view + command contribution diffs)\n- Spec sections this phase claims to satisfy: `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (hard limits, error handling, single source of truth), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` (all sections — §3 accessibility, §6 lists & tables, §7 modals, §8 state management, §11 visual standards, §14 i18n, §16 telemetry), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`.\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Open every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm the eleven operator views from spec §10 are all reachable from `ReviewPanelProvider`: memory inbox, proposed promotions, proposed contradictions, stale memory list, memory evidence view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, usefulness metrics, workspace graph health, indexing health, consolidation queue. (Usefulness metrics may live in the existing sidebar from `sidebar.ts` if R70 confirmed that surface; either way the link must be reachable.)\n2. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for `window.confirm(`, `window.prompt(`, `window.alert(` — every hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §7. Grep for `<dialog` to confirm the modals from T73 and T75 use native `<dialog>` elements.\n3. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for inline English literals in JSX-equivalent strings: look for quoted strings inside webview HTML template literals that are not wrapped in `i18n.t(...)`. Any hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §14.\n4. Open `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` and confirm namespaces exist for: panel, statusBadge, memoryInbox, promotionQueue, contradictionQueue, proposalDialog, staleView, evidenceInspector, eventTraceView, retrievalExplanationView, consolidationQueueView, indexingHealthView, workspaceGraphHealthView. Spot-check that every column header, action label, empty state, and error state from the eleven views has a corresponding key.\n5. Open every mutation handler (accept/reject in `ProposalDialog`, re-verify in `staleView`/`evidenceInspector`, retry in `consolidationQueueView`, recompute in `workspaceGraphHealthView`) and confirm each is wrapped in `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` plus an inline error state. Silent `.catch(() => {})` or bare `await` without surrounding handler is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §8.5 and `coding.md` §Error handling.\n6. Confirm `StatusBadge.ts` covers all eight verification statuses from spec §8 (`verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`) and every additional badge family (proposal status, job status, decision badge in retrieval explanation). Confirm the file is the only place `verification-status → color/label` mapping lives.\n7. Open every table (memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace, candidate table in retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, per-family counts in graph health) and confirm sortable columns use the `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents exposed by T71 (per `ui-specification.md` §6.2). Confirm pagination is present on every list per `ui-specification.md` §6.1.\n8. Open every interactive surface and confirm it renders loading, empty, and error states explicitly (per `ui-specification.md` §8.2, §8.3, §8.5).\n9. Grep for `prompt(`, raw `<select>`, and `type=\"number\"` ID inputs — any hit for ID-bearing fields is a blocker. Confirm filter pickers use `vscode.window.showQuickPick`.\n10. Run `wc -l` on every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm no file exceeds 800 lines per `coding.md`.\n11. Grep all files for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` without justification — each hit is a blocker per `coding.md` §No broken windows.\n12. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint && npm test` and confirm everything passes.\n13. Cross-check that the smoke tests from T77 cover every subview from T72–T76 (activation, view registration, mount, empty data, bridge error).\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Spec alignment` — phase-by-phase coverage matrix for spec §10: one row per required operator view (eleven views), evidence column citing the file/function that delivers the view, status PASS / PASS-WITH-CAVEATS / BLOCK.\n- `## Coding-standard alignment` — line-by-line audit vs. `coding.md` hard limits: file lengths (`wc -l` table for every Phase 10 file), function-length grep, forbidden-token grep (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, unjustified suppressions), error-handling audit (every mutation `try/catch` confirmed), single-source-of-truth audit (status-badge mapping in one place, i18n catalog as single source).\n- `## UI-specification compliance` — walk every section of `ui-specification.md` that applies to the review UI in order. Each subsection states the rule, lists the artifacts checked, and the verdict:\n  - §3 Accessibility baseline — focus management, keyboard, ARIA, target size, focus rings, live regions on status badges.\n  - §6 Lists & tables — pagination present on every list; sortable headers via `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents; column standards.\n  - §7 Modals & dialogs — every modal native `<dialog>`; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`; typed-confirm on high-scope destructive actions in `ProposalDialog`.\n  - §8 State management — loading, empty, error states on every surface; every mutation has `try/catch` + `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`; error message style obeys Nielsen #9.\n  - §11 Visual standards — status badges use the single shared component; semantic color tokens, not hard-coded colors.\n  - §12 Data display — dates via `Intl.DateTimeFormat`; truncation on long content.\n  - §14 Internationalization — all user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)`; no inline literals; ICU placeholders on interpolated strings; no string concatenation for user text.\n  - §16 Telemetry-friendly markup — `data-testid` on every interactive element; format `data-testid=\"review-<view>-<action>\"`.\n- `## i18n coverage` — table of every user-facing string the review UI emits, with the i18n key and the namespace; flag any inline literal that escaped catalog wrapping as a blocker.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `grep -q '## Spec alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms spec alignment is a dedicated section, not prose.\n- `grep -q '## UI-specification compliance' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms UI-spec compliance walked explicitly.\n- `grep -q '## i18n coverage' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms i18n coverage table present.\n- `grep -q '## Verdict' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review concludes with a binding verdict.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Coding-standard alignment` and `## Findings` headings are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"92e01477-56ca-4f53-90e6-aa05679be5ea","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:797:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DeEj2PHYWT2tYJ3BkufH5S","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R79.md","content":"# R79 — Contract gate — Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI MCP plumbing\n\n**Phase:** Contract gate after Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R64, R78\n**Spec anchor:** [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface), [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§MCP Tool Contract Principles](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-tool-contract-principles)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review is the contract gate between the Phase 10 review UI and the daemon MCP surface that powers it. Every method on `ReviewRpcBridge` calls a tool registered on the daemon side; this gate verifies that every method has a matching tool, that the JSON shapes round-trip end to end, that the schema is documented and regression-tested, and that no surface added by Phases 6–9 was forgotten on the extension side. The gate exists because UI compile + lint cannot detect contract drift — only an integration test that sends a real request to a real daemon and asserts the response shape can. Spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties demands \"every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested\"; this review enforces that for the review UI surface.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77 (extension review surface).\n- Every artifact produced by Phase 6–9 MCP work the review UI depends on, including the R64 contract gate output for the MCP tool schema.\n- Existing files this review cross-checks:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — registered MCP tools.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed extension-side bridge.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — underlying JSON-RPC client.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (from T02) — backward-compatibility policy this gate enforces.\n- Spec sections: `## 9. MCP Surface`, `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (schema parity, single source of truth); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` (backward-compatibility discipline).\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Enumerate every method exposed by `ReviewRpcBridge` and confirm each maps to a registered MCP tool in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Any orphan method on either side is a blocker.\n2. For every mapped pair, inspect the typed request and response shapes on the TypeScript side (`rpcBridge.ts`) and the corresponding Rust schema in `mcp.rs` (and its serde structs). Field-by-field comparison: type, required/optional, name. Drift is a blocker per `coding.md` §Schema parity.\n3. Cross-check `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (T02) — every review-UI tool is either `stable`, `additive`, or `redesigned-with-shim`; nothing is undocumented.\n4. Create `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` (this is the deliverable contract test). The test launches a real daemon process via the existing pattern in `extension/src/test/`, sends one round-trip request per review-UI bridge method, and asserts the response parses into the typed shape. Include happy-path requests and an error-path request per tool. Tests use the real daemon binary (`daemon/target/release/lattice`), not a mock.\n5. Run the contract suite with `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm test`. Confirm every round-trip passes. If the daemon binary is not present in `extension/bin/`, the test setup builds and copies per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy: `cd daemon && cargo build --release && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/`.\n6. Confirm the contract test exercises every review-UI tool from `rpcBridge.ts`: memory listing, evidence inspection, promotion queue read + apply + reject, contradiction queue read + apply + reject, stale listing, verify_memory, event trace read, retrieval explanation read, consolidation queue read + retry, indexing health read, graph health read + recompute.\n7. Grep `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` for the corresponding tool registrations and confirm each accepts the documented request shape and returns the documented response shape.\n8. Cross-check that backward-compat shims from R64 (if any) are still in place: every legacy tool name continues to route to the new implementation.\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Contract surfaces` — table of every `ReviewRpcBridge` method, the corresponding daemon tool name, the request shape, the response shape, and the spec citation. One row per method.\n- `## Round-trip evidence` — for every method, the contract-test name in `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` that exercises it; the test output snippet showing the request, the response, and the assertion that the typed shape parsed; the daemon log line showing the request was received.\n- `## Schema surfaces` — TypeScript ↔ Rust field comparison table per tool. Columns: TS field, TS type, TS required, Rust field, Rust type, Rust required, parity verdict. Any drift is flagged.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `test -f extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` — confirms the new contract test file exists.\n- `grep -q '## Contract surfaces' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms contract surfaces enumerated under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Round-trip evidence' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms round-trip evidence is present.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile (including the new contract test) succeeds.\n- `cd extension && npm test` — the contract suite plus all prior tests pass against a real daemon.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` exists, compiles, and passes against the real daemon binary.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Schema surfaces` and `## Findings` are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"ce6e8520-9b5f-42b8-a09b-8be0a2ce22df","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:867:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01MPB6E3V4a7oByv1GVt2tnV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013HQsYfpzN9F19m4z35f4hY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T69.md","content":"# T69 — Phase 9 — Metrics regression coverage tests (success-criteria targets from spec)\n\n**Phase:** 9\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T65, T66, T67, T68\n**Spec anchor:** [§Measurable Success Criteria](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#measurable-success-criteria)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task ships the regression tests that prove the cognitive workspace fork beats the baselines on every initial target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria. The tests run the redesigned tools (T56/T57) against the T66 fixture repos, feed the captured events into the T65 metrics collector, render the T68 regression report, and assert each spec §Measurable Success Criteria target. Without this task, the metrics infrastructure exists but no automated gate proves the system meets its stated goals — the Phase 9 DoD (\"changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\") would not be satisfied.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Initial targets: 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks; 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads; 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks; zero trusted display of known contradicted memory; zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label; 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks.\" (spec §Measurable Success Criteria)\n\n> \"Definition of done: changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote.\" (spec §Phase 9)\n\n> \"The fork should beat current Lattice on: lower tool calls per coding task; lower irrelevant context inclusion; higher relevant file/symbol recall; higher memory reuse precision; lower stale memory surfacing; better repeated-failure avoidance; better test recommendation accuracy; better user correction retention; faster diagnosis from failures; more explainable context selection.\" (spec §Measurable Success Criteria)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T65 — `MetricsCollector`, `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`.\n  - T66 — fixture repos + `golden_anchors.json` + `cognitive_workspace_benchmark`.\n  - T67 — `metrics_surface` (per-call relevance is needed for `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask` and inclusion-precision assertions).\n  - T68 — `RegressionReport`, `SuccessCriteriaThresholds`, `lattice_report` binary.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/baseline_metrics.json` (from T04) — baseline column for delta-based assertions (30% fewer, 40% fewer).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/` — module from T65.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/` and `memory_v2/` — tools under test.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits`, `## Tests as documentation`, `## No broken windows`, `## Schema / model parity`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Measurable Success Criteria and §Phase 9 DoD in full. Quote the \"Initial targets\" paragraph verbatim into a module-level doc comment on `metrics/regression_tests.rs` so the thresholds are visible at the test source.\n2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/regression_tests.rs` containing one `#[test]` per spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial target. Each test loads the T66 fixtures, runs the redesigned tools end-to-end against the golden tasks, feeds events into the T65 `MetricsCollector`, and asserts the corresponding threshold using the T68 `SuccessCriteriaThresholds`:\n   - `test_discovery_tool_calls_at_least_30pct_below_baseline` — `ToolCallsPerSuccessfulTask` is ≥30% below the baseline value from `baseline_metrics.json` for the discovery-tool subset.\n   - `test_irrelevant_file_reads_at_least_40pct_below_baseline` — `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask` is ≥40% below baseline.\n   - `test_memory_inclusion_precision_at_least_80pct` — `MemoryInclusionPrecision` ≥ 0.80 on the curated memory benchmark tasks from T66.\n   - `test_zero_trusted_display_of_contradicted_memory` — `ContradictionMissedRate` equals 0 across all fixture tasks.\n   - `test_zero_trusted_display_of_stale_memory_without_label` — `StaleMemorySurfacedRate` equals 0 across all fixture tasks (stale memories may appear, but never without an explicit stale label per spec §Phase 7 DoD).\n   - `test_relevant_test_recommendation_at_least_90pct` — `TestsRecommendedVsNeeded` recall ≥ 0.90 on the curated tasks from T66.\n3. Each test must surface a useful failure diagnostic: when an assertion fails, print the per-signal `MetricValue` plus the contributing event/memory evidence pointers from T68 so the regression is investigable without re-running.\n4. Mark long-running tests `#[ignore]` only if they exceed CI budget; the verification command runs with `-- --include-ignored` so CI executes them. Spec §Tests as documentation forbids silently skipping required coverage.\n5. Implement a helper `MetricsTestHarness` in the same file (or a sibling `regression_harness.rs`) that loads fixtures deterministically, executes a fixed tool sequence per task, captures events into an isolated `.lattice/events.db`, and returns the resulting `MetricValue`s. The harness encapsulates the deterministic-seed and fixture-freeze policy from T66 so per-test code is short.\n6. Add an explicit assertion at the top of the file that `baseline_metrics.json` exists; if not, the tests fail fast with a clear error pointing to T04 rather than passing accidentally.\n7. Workspace boundary + scope: every harness invocation runs in an isolated workspace under a temp directory; never reuses or contaminates the developer's local `.lattice/`.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/regression_tests.rs` — one `#[test]` per spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial target asserting the redesigned system beats baselines (30% discovery, 40% irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision, 0 stale/contradicted trusted display, 90% test recommendation); shared `MetricsTestHarness` for deterministic fixture execution; per-failure diagnostics via T68 evidence pointers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/regression_tests.rs` — confirms the regression-tests file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib metrics::regression_tests -- --include-ignored` — every spec §Measurable Success Criteria regression test passes against the T66 fixture repos.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (split into `regression_tests.rs` + `regression_harness.rs` if needed).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: each test reads thresholds from the T68 `SuccessCriteriaThresholds` rather than hard-coding numbers in two places; signal names use the T65 `MetricSignal` enum.\n- No silent error handling — every fixture load / harness failure produces a typed error; no test uses `unwrap()` on a `Result` that could legitimately fail without diagnostic.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no `#[ignore]` without a written reason; no skipped assertions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`MetricsTestHarness::run_fixture_task`, not `MetricsTestHarness::go`).\n- For SQLite: temp DBs only; harness cleans up on completion.\n- Observability: each test emits `tracing::info!` with `signal`, `target`, `actual`, `pass` fields on completion.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Measurable Success Criteria` and `## Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the six spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial targets has a corresponding `#[test]`; no target is punted with a placeholder.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3556,"cache_read_input_tokens":106980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3556,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"3f844d71-255e-43d5-a676-4efcaa90f907","request_id":"req_011Cb7NbRkgeaFKnUV3Q7p7E","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
