docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2004:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4057:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T36.md:42:4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs`: a small module exposing `emit_memory_retrieved(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)` and `emit_memory_expanded(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)`. Each function computes the diff (which identities were added to `selected_memories`, which were added to `excluded_memories` with reasons), packages the diff into an event payload per Phase 2's event-payload contract (workspace, branch, session, task, actor, timestamp, payload hash, compact summary, optional full payload location), and writes through the Phase 2 event writer.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2005:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4059:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T36.md:46:8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` covering: tool registration appears in `tools/list`; compact mode returns a summary; diagnostic mode returns the full state and embedded `RetrievalBundle`-shaped `selected_memories`; `include_excluded=true` returns excluded entries with reasons; expansion handle round-trips back to the same state; a synthetic event writer captures one `MemoryRetrieved` event per `retrieve` call and one `MemoryExpanded` event per `expand` call; event payload carries the included and excluded identity diffs and reasons.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2016:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4154:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R05.log:11:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013SN72EXjZoKVV7PgLUBYLw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cb46183-2e7c-4dbc-b888-4ae03f7bba08","uuid":"d5e243ea-3d28-4697-91ea-bce60d5527b4","timestamp":"2026-05-17T03:27:41.328Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-16\n**Status:** Proposed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n\nThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n\nThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n\n- code graph\n- document graph\n- event log\n- typed memory graph\n- workflow engine\n- consolidation engine\n- verification engine\n- explainable retrieval and ranking\n- assistant usefulness metrics\n\nThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n\n## Design Thesis\n\nThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n\nThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n\n## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n\n### A-Mem\n\nA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n\n- atomic memory notes\n- generated context, tags, and keywords\n- embeddings for semantic access\n- explicit memory-to-memory links\n- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n\nAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n\n### AgeMem\n\nAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n\n- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n\nDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2017:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4156:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R05.log:20:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012RienWzWHFrkdLBmCo9QtQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Crate Boundary Plan\n2\t\n3\tThis plan is the binding Phase 0 crate and module layout for the cognitive workspace branch. It follows the in-place extension decision in [2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md](./2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md) and the substrate overview in [2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md](./2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md).\n4\t\n5\tThe plan cites the fork spec's [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design) and [## Fork Strategy](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#fork-strategy) headings directly. It also preserves the current storage/search contract described in [2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md#Contract-Notes](./2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md#contract-notes) instead of duplicating that note.\n6\t\n7\t## Decision\n8\t\n9\tPhase 1 through Phase 7 will land as modules inside `lattice-core`, not as immediate sibling crates. The reserved crate names are still part of the contract: `lattice-identity`, `lattice-events`, `lattice-memory`, `lattice-retrieval`, `lattice-working-memory`, `lattice-consolidation`, and `lattice-verification`.\n10\t\n11\tThe in-core choice is deliberate:\n12\t\n13\t- `lattice-core` already owns the graph, storage, query, memory, indexing, and workflow types that these substrates must share.\n14\t- The downstream Phase 1 and Phase 2 task files already target `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/` and `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/`.\n15\t- Keeping the substrates in one crate avoids premature public-API stabilization while identities, events, memory links, and ranking contracts are still being shaped.\n16\t- The module boundaries below are extraction-ready. A later extraction to the reserved crate names is allowed only after the public API, storage migrations, and MCP compatibility shims are stable and a review proves no dependency cycle would be introduced.\n17\t\n18\t## `lattice-core::identity`\n19\t\n20\tReserved crate name: `lattice-identity`.\n21\t\n22\tPurpose: Own stable typed identities for files, directories, symbols, docs, sections, tests, events, memories, context handles, and runtime surfaces. It generalizes the existing `SymbolId` basis without replacing the current graph primary key.\n23\t\n24\tPublic API surface:\n25\t\n26\t- identity value types and typed id encodings\n27\t- identity parser and serializer for MCP payloads\n28\t- resolver traits for path, symbol, heading, test, memory, event, and handle references\n29\t- ambiguity diagnostics for duplicate or stale names\n30\t- compatibility helpers for legacy name-based references\n31\t\n32\tAllowed dependencies:\n33\t\n34\t- `lattice-core::symbols`\n35\t- `lattice-core::graph`\n36\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n37\t- `lattice-core::workspace`\n38\t- `lattice-core::parser` for doc section and test identity extraction\n39\t\n40\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and [## Fork Strategy](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#fork-strategy).\n41\t\n42\tBoundary rationale: Identity sits below every later substrate, so it must not depend on events, memory, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, or verification. That keeps the dependency graph acyclic and preserves current graph and memory compatibility.\n43\t\n44\t## `lattice-core::events`\n45\t\n46\tReserved crate name: `lattice-events`.\n47\t\n48\tPurpose: Own the append-only event log, event payload hashing, replay readers, event compaction metadata, and workflow/session correlation. It records assistant and workflow activity through stable identities from `identity`.\n49\t\n50\tPublic API surface:\n51\t\n52\t- event kind taxonomy\n53\t- event envelope and referenced-identity fields\n54\t- append-only writer API\n55\t- bounded reader/query API\n56\t- replay cursors and snapshot coordination types\n57\t- payload hashing and spillover payload references\n58\t\n59\tAllowed dependencies:\n60\t\n61\t- `lattice-core::identity`\n62\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n63\t- `lattice-core::workspace`\n64\t- `lattice-core::memory` only through narrow memory-id references, not memory retrieval\n65\t\n66\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-2-event-log-substrate), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and [## Fork Strategy](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#fork-strategy).\n67\t\n68\tBoundary rationale: Events depend on identity and storage but remain below memory consolidation and retrieval. Replay readers may produce graph or memory rebuild inputs; they do not call high-level workflow code.\n69\t\n70\t## `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n71\t\n72\tReserved crate name: `lattice-memory`.\n73\t\n74\tPurpose: Own normalized memory classes, links, evidence, accesses, scores, contradiction/supersession semantics, and migration adapters from the existing `lattice-core::memory` row model.\n75\t\n76\tPublic API surface:\n77\t\n78\t- memory class taxonomy including `CounterMemory`\n79\t- memory link and evidence models\n80\t- memory access and score writers\n81\t- migration/backfill helpers from existing `memories` rows\n82\t- trust, freshness, contradiction, and supersession queries\n83\t- compatibility adapters for current observation-oriented MCP tools\n84\t\n85\tAllowed dependencies:\n86\t\n87\t- `lattice-core::identity`\n88\t- `lattice-core::events`\n89\t- `lattice-core::memory`\n90\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n91\t- `lattice-core::workspace`\n92\t\n93\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-3-memory-graph-storage), [### 4. Memory Graph](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n94\t\n95\tBoundary rationale: The module wraps the existing memory store instead of forking it. Existing `memories` rows remain the compatibility table while normalized side tables become the first-class model for new behavior.\n96\t\n97\t## `lattice-core::retrieval`\n98\t\n99\tReserved crate name: `lattice-retrieval`.\n100\t\n101\tPurpose: Own Retrieval V1: task intent, anchor extraction, hybrid candidate retrieval, explainable scoring, compact/full response shaping, and diagnostic ranking reasons across graph, docs, memories, and events.\n102\t\n103\tPublic API surface:\n104\t\n105\t- task-intent classifier\n106\t- anchor extractor and resolver integration\n107\t- candidate model across graph, docs, events, and memories\n108\t- scoring configuration and score explanations\n109\t- compact and diagnostic bundle shapers\n110\t- benchmark fixture entry points\n111\t\n112\tAllowed dependencies:\n113\t\n114\t- `lattice-core::identity`\n115\t- `lattice-core::events`\n116\t- `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n117\t- `lattice-core::graph`\n118\t- `lattice-core::query`\n119\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n120\t- `lattice-core::intelligence` only through adapter traits during migration\n121\t\n122\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [## MCP Tool Contract Principles](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-tool-contract-principles), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n123\t\n124\tBoundary rationale: Retrieval is above graph, event, memory, and identity storage. It must not be called by those lower modules, or the shared substrates would become cyclic and hard to test.\n125\t\n126\t## `lattice-core::working_memory`\n127\t\n128\tReserved crate name: `lattice-working-memory`.\n129\t\n130\tPurpose: Own explicit per-task working memory state, included/excluded context bookkeeping, pins, evictions, expansion state, compression state, and checkpoint restore.\n131\t\n132\tPublic API surface:\n133\t\n134\t- task working-memory state model\n135\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n136\t- checkpoint serializer and restore reader\n137\t- context-handle integration types\n138\t- MCP inspection payload models\n139\t\n140\tAllowed dependencies:\n141\t\n142\t- `lattice-core::identity`\n143\t- `lattice-core::events`\n144\t- `lattice-core::retrieval`\n145\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n146\t- `lattice-core::intelligence` only through workflow adapters\n147\t\n148\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 5: Working Memory](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [## MCP Surface](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-surface), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n149\t\n150\tBoundary rationale: Working memory consumes retrieval results and emits events. It is task-local state, so it must not own durable memory graph semantics or identity resolution rules.\n151\t\n152\t## `lattice-core::consolidation`\n153\t\n154\tReserved crate name: `lattice-consolidation`.\n155\t\n156\tPurpose: Own background consolidation jobs, episode summaries, memory promotion proposals, duplicate detection, contradiction detection, supersession proposals, procedure extraction, failure-pattern extraction, and review-queue handoff.\n157\t\n158\tPublic API surface:\n159\t\n160\t- consolidation job model and lifecycle\n161\t- replay-safe job runner traits\n162\t- deterministic proposal generation helpers\n163\t- LLM proposal metadata with model, prompt hash, and response hash\n164\t- review queue payload types\n165\t- reversible job outcome records\n166\t\n167\tAllowed dependencies:\n168\t\n169\t- `lattice-core::identity`\n170\t- `lattice-core::events`\n171\t- `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n172\t- `lattice-core::retrieval`\n173\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n174\t- `lattice-core::workspace`\n175\t\n176\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-6-consolidation-engine), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and the event compaction requirement under [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n177\t\n178\tBoundary rationale: Consolidation is background work over events and memories. It may propose memory changes but must not silently bypass memory graph validation or verification.\n179\t\n180\t## `lattice-core::verification`\n181\t\n182\tReserved crate name: `lattice-verification`.\n183\t\n184\tPurpose: Own incremental memory verification, file/symbol/doc/test existence checks, exact-span evidence validation, branch/workspace scope enforcement, expiry, stale-memory surfacing, and verification diagnostics.\n185\t\n186\tPublic API surface:\n187\t\n188\t- verification job model and lifecycle\n189\t- evidence validators for identity-backed references\n190\t- freshness and expiry evaluators\n191\t- stale, contradicted, superseded, and invalidated status transitions\n192\t- operator diagnostic payloads\n193\t- incremental graph-change verification hooks\n194\t\n195\tAllowed dependencies:\n196\t\n197\t- `lattice-core::identity`\n198\t- `lattice-core::events`\n199\t- `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n200\t- `lattice-core::graph`\n201\t- `lattice-core::storage`\n202\t- `lattice-core::workspace`\n203\t\n204\tSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-7-verification-and-freshness), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and the trust rules in [### 4. Memory Graph](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph).\n205\t\n206\tBoundary rationale: Verification consumes graph facts and memory evidence. It writes verification results and events, but retrieval only reads those results; retrieval must not decide trust state itself.\n207\t\n208\t## Dependency graph\n209\t\n210\tThe Phase 1 through Phase 7 module graph is directed and acyclic:\n211\t\n212\t```text\n213\tlattice-daemon\n214\t  -> lattice-core::working_memory\n215\t  -> lattice-core::retrieval\n216\t  -> lattice-core::consolidation\n217\t  -> lattice-core::verification\n218\t  -> lattice-core::events\n219\t  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n220\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n221\t\n222\tlattice-core::consolidation\n223\t  -> lattice-core::retrieval\n224\t  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n225\t  -> lattice-core::events\n226\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n227\t\n228\tlattice-core::working_memory\n229\t  -> lattice-core::retrieval\n230\t  -> lattice-core::events\n231\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n232\t\n233\tlattice-core::retrieval\n234\t  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n235\t  -> lattice-core::events\n236\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n237\t\n238\tlattice-core::verification\n239\t  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n240\t  -> lattice-core::events\n241\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n242\t\n243\tlattice-core::memory_graph\n244\t  -> lattice-core::events\n245\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n246\t\n247\tlattice-core::events\n248\t  -> lattice-core::identity\n249\t\n250\tlattice-core::identity\n251\t  -> existing lattice-core graph, parser, storage, symbols, and workspace modules\n252\t```\n253\t\n254\tNo lower substrate may call a higher substrate. In particular, `identity` must not call `events`; `events` must not call `retrieval`, `working_memory`, `consolidation`, or `verification`; and `memory_graph` must not call `retrieval`.\n255\t\n256\t## Test boundary\n257\t\n258\tEach new module carries its own test modules under `src/` per the convention in [context.md#2-carry-forward-inventory-what-lattice-already-gives-us](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md#2-carry-forward-inventory-what-lattice-already-gives-us). Expected paths include:\n259\t\n260\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/tests.rs`\n261\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/tests.rs`\n262\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/tests.rs`\n263\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval/tests.rs`\n264\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/tests.rs`\n265\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/tests.rs`\n266\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/tests.rs`\n267\t\n268\tModule tests own local invariants. Cross-module behavior belongs in the highest module that composes the behavior, with daemon/MCP compatibility tests only at the RPC boundary.\n269\t\n270\t## Extraction criteria\n271\t\n272\tA module may move to its reserved crate name only when all of these are true:\n273\t\n274\t- its public API has stopped changing for one full numbered phase\n275\t- all storage migrations it owns are documented in the storage migration policy\n276\t- all MCP compatibility shims affected by the move are documented and tested\n277\t- the resulting Cargo graph remains acyclic\n278\t- the crate has its own focused test target and does not require `lattice-daemon` to validate core invariants\n279\t\n280\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cb46183-2e7c-4dbc-b888-4ae03f7bba08","uuid":"9f233e14-beda-4957-ac15-4c8b83a7b389","timestamp":"2026-05-17T03:27:55.666Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/architecture/2026-05-16-crate-boundary-plan.md","content":"# Crate Boundary Plan\n\nThis plan is the binding Phase 0 crate and module layout for the cognitive workspace branch. It follows the in-place extension decision in [2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md](./2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md) and the substrate overview in [2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md](./2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md).\n\nThe plan cites the fork spec's [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design) and [## Fork Strategy](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#fork-strategy) headings directly. It also preserves the current storage/search contract described in [2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md#Contract-Notes](./2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md#contract-notes) instead of duplicating that note.\n\n## Decision\n\nPhase 1 through Phase 7 will land as modules inside `lattice-core`, not as immediate sibling crates. The reserved crate names are still part of the contract: `lattice-identity`, `lattice-events`, `lattice-memory`, `lattice-retrieval`, `lattice-working-memory`, `lattice-consolidation`, and `lattice-verification`.\n\nThe in-core choice is deliberate:\n\n- `lattice-core` already owns the graph, storage, query, memory, indexing, and workflow types that these substrates must share.\n- The downstream Phase 1 and Phase 2 task files already target `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/` and `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/`.\n- Keeping the substrates in one crate avoids premature public-API stabilization while identities, events, memory links, and ranking contracts are still being shaped.\n- The module boundaries below are extraction-ready. A later extraction to the reserved crate names is allowed only after the public API, storage migrations, and MCP compatibility shims are stable and a review proves no dependency cycle would be introduced.\n\n## `lattice-core::identity`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-identity`.\n\nPurpose: Own stable typed identities for files, directories, symbols, docs, sections, tests, events, memories, context handles, and runtime surfaces. It generalizes the existing `SymbolId` basis without replacing the current graph primary key.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- identity value types and typed id encodings\n- identity parser and serializer for MCP payloads\n- resolver traits for path, symbol, heading, test, memory, event, and handle references\n- ambiguity diagnostics for duplicate or stale names\n- compatibility helpers for legacy name-based references\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::symbols`\n- `lattice-core::graph`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::workspace`\n- `lattice-core::parser` for doc section and test identity extraction\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and [## Fork Strategy](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#fork-strategy).\n\nBoundary rationale: Identity sits below every later substrate, so it must not depend on events, memory, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, or verification. That keeps the dependency graph acyclic and preserves current graph and memory compatibility.\n\n## `lattice-core::events`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-events`.\n\nPurpose: Own the append-only event log, event payload hashing, replay readers, event compaction metadata, and workflow/session correlation. It records assistant and workflow activity through stable identities from `identity`.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- event kind taxonomy\n- event envelope and referenced-identity fields\n- append-only writer API\n- bounded reader/query API\n- replay cursors and snapshot coordination types\n- payload hashing and spillover payload references\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::identity`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::workspace`\n- `lattice-core::memory` only through narrow memory-id references, not memory retrieval\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-2-event-log-substrate), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and [## Fork Strategy](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#fork-strategy).\n\nBoundary rationale: Events depend on identity and storage but remain below memory consolidation and retrieval. Replay readers may produce graph or memory rebuild inputs; they do not call high-level workflow code.\n\n## `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-memory`.\n\nPurpose: Own normalized memory classes, links, evidence, accesses, scores, contradiction/supersession semantics, and migration adapters from the existing `lattice-core::memory` row model.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- memory class taxonomy including `CounterMemory`\n- memory link and evidence models\n- memory access and score writers\n- migration/backfill helpers from existing `memories` rows\n- trust, freshness, contradiction, and supersession queries\n- compatibility adapters for current observation-oriented MCP tools\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::identity`\n- `lattice-core::events`\n- `lattice-core::memory`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::workspace`\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-3-memory-graph-storage), [### 4. Memory Graph](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n\nBoundary rationale: The module wraps the existing memory store instead of forking it. Existing `memories` rows remain the compatibility table while normalized side tables become the first-class model for new behavior.\n\n## `lattice-core::retrieval`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-retrieval`.\n\nPurpose: Own Retrieval V1: task intent, anchor extraction, hybrid candidate retrieval, explainable scoring, compact/full response shaping, and diagnostic ranking reasons across graph, docs, memories, and events.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- task-intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver integration\n- candidate model across graph, docs, events, and memories\n- scoring configuration and score explanations\n- compact and diagnostic bundle shapers\n- benchmark fixture entry points\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::identity`\n- `lattice-core::events`\n- `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n- `lattice-core::graph`\n- `lattice-core::query`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::intelligence` only through adapter traits during migration\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [## MCP Tool Contract Principles](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-tool-contract-principles), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n\nBoundary rationale: Retrieval is above graph, event, memory, and identity storage. It must not be called by those lower modules, or the shared substrates would become cyclic and hard to test.\n\n## `lattice-core::working_memory`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-working-memory`.\n\nPurpose: Own explicit per-task working memory state, included/excluded context bookkeeping, pins, evictions, expansion state, compression state, and checkpoint restore.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- task working-memory state model\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- checkpoint serializer and restore reader\n- context-handle integration types\n- MCP inspection payload models\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::identity`\n- `lattice-core::events`\n- `lattice-core::retrieval`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::intelligence` only through workflow adapters\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 5: Working Memory](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [## MCP Surface](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-surface), and [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n\nBoundary rationale: Working memory consumes retrieval results and emits events. It is task-local state, so it must not own durable memory graph semantics or identity resolution rules.\n\n## `lattice-core::consolidation`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-consolidation`.\n\nPurpose: Own background consolidation jobs, episode summaries, memory promotion proposals, duplicate detection, contradiction detection, supersession proposals, procedure extraction, failure-pattern extraction, and review-queue handoff.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- consolidation job model and lifecycle\n- replay-safe job runner traits\n- deterministic proposal generation helpers\n- LLM proposal metadata with model, prompt hash, and response hash\n- review queue payload types\n- reversible job outcome records\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::identity`\n- `lattice-core::events`\n- `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n- `lattice-core::retrieval`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::workspace`\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-6-consolidation-engine), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and the event compaction requirement under [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design).\n\nBoundary rationale: Consolidation is background work over events and memories. It may propose memory changes but must not silently bypass memory graph validation or verification.\n\n## `lattice-core::verification`\n\nReserved crate name: `lattice-verification`.\n\nPurpose: Own incremental memory verification, file/symbol/doc/test existence checks, exact-span evidence validation, branch/workspace scope enforcement, expiry, stale-memory surfacing, and verification diagnostics.\n\nPublic API surface:\n\n- verification job model and lifecycle\n- evidence validators for identity-backed references\n- freshness and expiry evaluators\n- stale, contradicted, superseded, and invalidated status transitions\n- operator diagnostic payloads\n- incremental graph-change verification hooks\n\nAllowed dependencies:\n\n- `lattice-core::identity`\n- `lattice-core::events`\n- `lattice-core::memory_graph`\n- `lattice-core::graph`\n- `lattice-core::storage`\n- `lattice-core::workspace`\n\nSpec sections implemented: [### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-7-verification-and-freshness), [## Storage Design](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), and the trust rules in [### 4. Memory Graph](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph).\n\nBoundary rationale: Verification consumes graph facts and memory evidence. It writes verification results and events, but retrieval only reads those results; retrieval must not decide trust state itself.\n\n## Dependency graph\n\nThe Phase 1 through Phase 7 module graph is directed and acyclic:\n\n```text\nlattice-daemon\n  -> lattice-core::working_memory\n  -> lattice-core::retrieval\n  -> lattice-core::consolidation\n  -> lattice-core::verification\n  -> lattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::consolidation\n  -> lattice-core::retrieval\n  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n  -> lattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::working_memory\n  -> lattice-core::retrieval\n  -> lattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::retrieval\n  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n  -> lattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::verification\n  -> lattice-core::memory_graph\n  -> lattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::memory_graph\n  -> lattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::events\n  -> lattice-core::identity\n\nlattice-core::identity\n  -> existing lattice-core graph, parser, storage, symbols, and workspace modules\n```\n\nNo lower substrate may call a higher substrate. In particular, `identity` must not call `events`; `events` must not call `retrieval`, `working_memory`, `consolidation`, or `verification`; and `memory_graph` must not call `retrieval`.\n\n## Test boundary\n\nEach new module carries its own test modules under `src/` per the convention in [context.md#2-carry-forward-inventory-what-lattice-already-gives-us](../plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md#2-carry-forward-inventory-what-lattice-already-gives-us). Expected paths include:\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/tests.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/tests.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/tests.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval/tests.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/tests.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/tests.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/tests.rs`\n\nModule tests own local invariants. Cross-module behavior belongs in the highest module that composes the behavior, with daemon/MCP compatibility tests only at the RPC boundary.\n\n## Extraction criteria\n\nA module may move to its reserved crate name only when all of these are true:\n\n- its public API has stopped changing for one full numbered phase\n- all storage migrations it owns are documented in the storage migration policy\n- all MCP compatibility shims affected by the move are documented and tested\n- the resulting Cargo graph remains acyclic\n- the crate has its own focused test target and does not require `lattice-daemon` to validate core invariants\n\n","numLines":280,"startLine":1,"totalLines":280}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2018:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4159:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T03.log:2680:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:12:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QR57zz5vCjWm6Nh812Nykt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"50e142df-b3bd-4b49-9ccf-0fed1541f6a9","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:17:40.069Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-16\n**Status:** Proposed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n\nThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n\nThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n\n- code graph\n- document graph\n- event log\n- typed memory graph\n- workflow engine\n- consolidation engine\n- verification engine\n- explainable retrieval and ranking\n- assistant usefulness metrics\n\nThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n\n## Design Thesis\n\nThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n\nThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n\n## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n\n### A-Mem\n\nA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n\n- atomic memory notes\n- generated context, tags, and keywords\n- embeddings for semantic access\n- explicit memory-to-memory links\n- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n\nAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n\n### AgeMem\n\nAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n\n- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n\nDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2019:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4161:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T03.log:2725:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:111:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018zsq4mM4akuU6Hs8yDzpsz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0fa88678-a6cb-4169-8c73-9d1c7278b0f0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.168Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2023:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4189:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:12:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QR57zz5vCjWm6Nh812Nykt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"50e142df-b3bd-4b49-9ccf-0fed1541f6a9","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:17:40.069Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-16\n**Status:** Proposed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n\nThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n\nThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n\n- code graph\n- document graph\n- event log\n- typed memory graph\n- workflow engine\n- consolidation engine\n- verification engine\n- explainable retrieval and ranking\n- assistant usefulness metrics\n\nThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n\n## Design Thesis\n\nThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n\nThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n\n## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n\n### A-Mem\n\nA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n\n- atomic memory notes\n- generated context, tags, and keywords\n- embeddings for semantic access\n- explicit memory-to-memory links\n- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n\nAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n\n### AgeMem\n\nAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n\n- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n\nDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2024:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4191:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:111:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018zsq4mM4akuU6Hs8yDzpsz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing toopisodeMemory` rows for that task, then apply the proposal and assert the memory appears AND a `MemoryConsolidated` event is in the event log.\n4. Write `test_deterministic_supersession_proposal_apply_then_reject_round_trip` (covers T41): seed two overlapping memories, run `SupersessionCandidates::scan`, assert one `Supersede` proposal is pending, apply it, assert the older memory's status is `superseded` AND a `memory_links` edge points to the newer, then call `ReplayDriver::reverse(proposal_id)` and assert the status flips back and the link is gone.\n5. Write `test_llm_episode_job_well_formed_response_emits_proposal_with_provenance` (covers T42 + T43): script the fake driver to return a well-formed JSON episode summary, run `EpisodeSummaryJob::run` in `Background` mode, assert a proposal exists with `provenance.is_some()`, `provenance.model == \"fake-llm\"`, and matching `prompt_sha256` / `response_sha256`.\n6. Write `test_llm_malformed_response_leaves_memory_unchanged_and_emits_failure_event` (covers T42 malformed-handling): script the driver to return invalid JSON, assert zero proposals enqueued, zero memory mutations, and exactly one `ConsolidationFailed` event with `error_kind = MalformedResponse`.\n7. Write `test_llm_synchronous_mode_is_forbidden_and_driver_never_called` (covers T42 mode guard): attempt `EpisodeSummaryJob::run(ctx { mode: SynchronousPostTask }, …)`, assert it returns `Err(ForbiddenOnHotPath)`, assert the fake driver's call count is zero.\n8. Write `test_bounded_queue_overflow_drops_jobs_with_warning_event` (covers T39 + T43): set `max_depth = 2`, enqueue 5 jobs of the same LLM kind, assert exactly 3 are dropped, exactly 3 `ConsolidationFailed` events with `error_kind = QueueFull` are emitted, and the `tracing::warn!` log surfaced (capture via a tracing-test subscriber).\n9. Write `test_high_scope_proposal_routes_to_manual_review_queue` (covers T44): seed a `repo`-scope target memory, run a job that produces a proposal for it, assert the proposal is `pending` and visible via `ReviewQueue::list_pending(scope=Repo)`, assert it did NOT auto-apply.\n10. Write `test_replay_from_genesis_reconstructs_memory_state` (covers T45): apply 10 mixed proposals through the runtime, snapshot the memory store hash, reset the memory tables, run `ReplayDriver::replay(FromGenesis)` with the provenance cache, assert the post-replay memory hash equals the snapshot hash AND the live LLM driver was never called.\n11. Wire the suite via `mod integration_tests;` in `consolidation/mod.rs` under `#[cfg(test)]` (or a `tests/` sibling per crate convention — match `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/tests.rs` style).\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/integration_tests.rs` — `ConsolidationHarness` plus the named behavior-per-test functions above covering synchronous, deterministic, LLM well-formed, LLM malformed, mode guard, queue overflow, manual-review routing, and replay reconstruction.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/integration_tests.rs` — confirms the integration test file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib consolidation::integration_tests` — every Phase 6 integration test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split harness into `integration_test_support.rs` if approaching the ceiling).\n- Test length ≤30 lines per `#[test]` function; setup factored into the harness.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 for harness helpers.\n- Single source of truth: tests exercise the real T39–T45 modules via their public APIs; no shadow re-implementation of proposal storage or event emission.\n- Naming: tests follow `test_<behavior_description>` per `coding.md` §Tests as documentation (e.g. `test_llm_malformed_response_leaves_memory_unchanged_and_emits_failure_event`).\n- Error handling: tests assert on typed outcomes (`EnqueueOutcome::Dropped`, `LlmJobError::ForbiddenOnHotPath`) rather than message strings.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Observability: tests capture `tracing::warn!` output via a test subscriber so warn-on-drop is verifiable, not just assumed.\n- Doc citation: file-top doc comment cites the four spec invariants verbatim (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] `integration_tests.rs` exists with every test from steps 3–10 implemented.\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied (in particular, no individual test exceeds 30 lines).\n- [ ] No deferred work: all four Phase 6 invariants are covered end-to-end in this task; no \"leave for later\" placeholders.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15541,"cache_read_input_tokens":56911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15541,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015qAoQeXM7HFtFDzBbPkUNq","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"abe467b8-23c3-46c2-8c75-07c0acef06a4","request_id":"req_011Cb7N1yvFZe4LypqRsfPgL","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 6+7+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2025:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4192:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:788:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013zgGbBpL5RCG9X6xGX3GFk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LLzhtrw7NNpnBWtrst5QqD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T36.md","content":"# T36 — Phase 5 — MCP surface for working memory inspection + event capture for include/exclude reasons\n\n**Phase:** 5\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T34, T35\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [§5. Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#5-working-memory), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log), [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the MCP tool that lets a client inspect the current `WorkingMemoryState` and the event-capture hooks that automatically log included and excluded context per spec §Phase 5 deliverable (\"automatic event capture for included and excluded context\"). The MCP tool exposes the same compact-by-default shape the rest of the MCP surface uses; the event hooks emit `MemoryRetrieved` and `MemoryExpanded` events (Phase 2 event kinds) every time the working memory's `selected_memories` or `excluded_memories` change. Together these make working memory observable — meeting the Phase 5 Definition of Done (\"workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\").\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: ... MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context ...\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Required event types: ... ContextBundleReturned; MemoryRetrieved; MemoryExpanded ...\" (spec §3. Event Log)\n\n> \"Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Every tool response should support: compact rendering; full structured JSON; context handles; stable expansion targets; budget controls.\" (spec §9. MCP Surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T34 `WorkingMemoryState` and checkpoint API.\n  - T35 `OpContext`, eight operations, and `record_state_mutation` internal hook.\n  - Phase 2 event-log API — exposes `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `ContextBundleReturned` writers.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server registration.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` dispatch and tool-schema declarations.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` — context-handle convention for round-trippable handles.\n  - Phase 2 event writer (assumed to live at `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/`).\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, single source of truth — reuse the existing MCP dispatch convention); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (compact-by-default; build/deploy procedure for the daemon).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Phase 5 deliverables, §3. Event Log event-kind list, and §9. MCP Surface response contract to fix the tool shape and event-emission contract.\n2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`: register a new MCP tool `inspect_working_memory` with schema `{ task_id: string, mode: \"compact\" | \"diagnostic\", include_excluded: boolean }`. The handler loads the active `WorkingMemoryState` for the task (from the session-scoped in-memory store maintained by T35's `OpContext`, falling back to the latest checkpoint if the session has rolled), then returns either a compact summary (via T35 `summarize`) or the full state including `excluded_memories` when `include_excluded=true`. Response carries an `expansion_handle` per spec §9 so the caller can expand the full state without re-fetching.\n3. Wire the tool into `mcp.rs` dispatch alongside the existing 43 tools. Tool registration includes the schema in the MCP `tools/list` response; failure to expose a parameter is a contract violation caught by the cross-layer MCP schema test gate.\n4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs`: a small module exposing `emit_memory_retrieved(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)` and `emit_memory_expanded(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)`. Each function computes the diff (which identities were added to `selected_memories`, which were added to `excluded_memories` with reasons), packages the diff into an event payload per Phase 2's event-payload contract (workspace, branch, session, task, actor, timestamp, payload hash, compact summary, optional full payload location), and writes through the Phase 2 event writer.\n5. Attach the hooks to T35's `record_state_mutation` so every state-mutating operation emits the appropriate event automatically: `retrieve` and `compress` emit `MemoryRetrieved` (since they decide what is included/excluded); `expand` emits `MemoryExpanded`; `pin`, `evict`, `filter`, `checkpoint` emit `MemoryRetrieved` when they change inclusion/exclusion. `summarize` emits no event.\n6. Honor the spec invariant \"the full event stream should not be injected into prompts\" — the event hooks never put event data back into `WorkingMemoryState`. Events flow one way: state mutation → event log.\n7. Hot-path discipline: event writes share the existing Phase 2 write budget (`≤5ms P99`). If the writer returns a backpressure error, the operation does not fail — the event is dropped with a `warn!` log naming the task and operation, per `coding.md` \"no silent .catch\" but tolerant of event-log overload.\n8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` covering: tool registration appears in `tools/list`; compact mode returns a summary; diagnostic mode returns the full state and embedded `RetrievalBundle`-shaped `selected_memories`; `include_excluded=true` returns excluded entries with reasons; expansion handle round-trips back to the same state; a synthetic event writer captures one `MemoryRetrieved` event per `retrieve` call and one `MemoryExpanded` event per `expand` call; event payload carries the included and excluded identity diffs and reasons.\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` (release build verifies the whole daemon still compiles with the new tool registered), then `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::working_memory_tool_tests`. Deploy the new daemon binary per the project rule (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`) only when explicitly told to ship; the test commands themselves do not require a redeploy.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs` — MCP tool registration, schema, handler, dispatch wiring.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs` — `emit_memory_retrieved`, `emit_memory_expanded`, state-diff computation, event payload assembly.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for tool registration, compact/diagnostic modes, exclude visibility, expansion handle, event capture.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release`\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::working_memory_tool_tests`\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. The MCP handler decomposes mode handling into helpers; event-diff computation is its own function.\n- Schema parity: the MCP tool JSON schema mirrors the Rust handler's parameter struct exactly; the response shape mirrors `WorkingMemoryState` (compact view exposes a documented subset). Schema declaration and Rust struct are updated in the same commit.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: state loading uses T34's `load_checkpoint` / T35's `OpContext` session store; no parallel state lookup. Event writing uses the Phase 2 event writer; no parallel event payload assembler.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed; the backpressure-drop path logs at `warn!` with task and operation context, never silent.\n- Doc citation: tool comments cite `## 9. MCP Surface`; event-hook comments cite `## 3. Event Log` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: tool registration, compact/diagnostic modes, exclude visibility, expansion handle, automatic event capture all delivered in this task.\n- [ ] Every Phase-5 state mutation emits an event without manual call sites — the `record_state_mutation` hook is the single attachment point.\n- [ ] Backpressure on the event log degrades to a logged drop, not a failed operation.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3458,"cache_read_input_tokens":74783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3458,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"4430d74f-dcef-44ba-9df8-04d1b33b60f6","request_id":"req_011Cb7NHj1kpRYTTxArY2nhh","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2026:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4196:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:815:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01FdMLjaXcZQEvJnpbkscPQS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012fr4bjgjxamqXhLwBA43HP","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R38.md","content":"# R38 — Backend review — Phase 5 working memory\n\n**Phase:** 5\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T34, T35, T36, T37\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [§5. Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#5-working-memory), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis review certifies that Phase 5 — Working Memory — is complete, spec-faithful, and ships the excluded-context auditability that makes working memory trustworthy. The reviewer reads every file produced by T34–T37, runs the verification commands, and writes a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md`. The report uses the headings required by the verification commands so the build harness can confirm it was actually written, not stubbed. Verdict is one of `Pass`, `Pass with follow-ups`, or `Fail with required rework`; `Fail` blocks the Phase 6 consolidation work from starting.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this review\n\n> \"Working memory contains: task statement; interpreted intent; active files and symbols; active hypotheses; active failures; current plan; selected memories; excluded memories and reasons; budget decisions; unresolved questions; verification status.\" (spec §5. Working Memory)\n\n> \"Required operations: retrieve; summarize; filter; pin; evict; expand; compress; checkpoint.\" (spec §5. Working Memory)\n\n> \"Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: explicit per-task working memory state; operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint; MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context; tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore. Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: every artifact produced by T34 (state + checkpoint table), T35 (operations), T36 (MCP tool + event hooks), T37 (test families).\n- Existing files this review reads:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs`, `state.rs`, `schema.sql`, `state_tests.rs`\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/operations.rs`, `operations_tests.rs`\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs`\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`, `working_memory_tool_tests.rs`\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/budget_tests.rs`, `checkpoint_tests.rs`\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`; `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`; `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §5. Working Memory, §Phase 5: Working Memory, and the relevant lines of §3. Event Log in full.\n2. Run every verification command from T34–T37 in sequence and record pass/fail with output excerpts in the review's `## Findings` section.\n3. Audit field coverage: open `WorkingMemoryState` and confirm every one of the eleven spec-named fields is present with the correct nullability and type; flag any field that was renamed or merged.\n4. Audit operation coverage: confirm each of the eight required operations exists as a callable, has a test in `operations_tests.rs`, honors the documented contract (idempotency, pin protection, no-op handling, etc.), and emits the appropriate event via T36's hook.\n5. Audit excluded-context auditability — the heart of this review:\n   - Confirm `ExcludedMemory.exclusion_reason` is non-optional and that every drop/skip path across T35 operations writes a populated reason (sample at least one entry from each of `filter`, `evict`, `compress`).\n   - Run the MCP `inspect_working_memory` tool against a synthetic state with mixed include/exclude entries; confirm `include_excluded=true` returns the excluded entries with reasons; confirm `include_excluded=false` does not silently leak them in a separate field.\n   - Confirm event hooks emit `MemoryRetrieved` / `MemoryExpanded` events whose payloads carry the included and excluded identity diffs with reasons. Sample at least three event payloads from the test capture.\n6. Audit checkpoint integrity: confirm `working_memory_checkpoints` schema has the documented columns and indexes; confirm save/load round-trip is byte-identical via canonical-JSON SHA-256; confirm version mismatch is rejected.\n7. Audit MCP surface compliance with spec §9: tool registration appears in `tools/list`; response supports compact and full modes; an `expansion_handle` is included; budget controls are respected.\n8. Audit coding-standard compliance across all produced files: file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, no TODOs/FIXMEs/commented-out code/unjustified `#[allow(...)]`, single source of truth (no parallel state loader, no parallel event writer, no fixtures duplicated with T32).\n9. Cross-check Phase 4 forward-compatibility actually held: confirm `RetrievalBundle` and `BundleResult` are embedded under `selected_memories` and `excluded_memories.result` unchanged from the Phase 4 schema; if a field was added or renamed, flag it as a Phase 4 rework requirement.\n10. Write the findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md` with the required headings; assign a verdict; list follow-ups (if any) as actionable items addressed to specific subsequent tasks.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md` — Phase 5 review report with required headings.\n\n## Required headings (verified by grep)\n\n- `## Spec alignment` — each spec deliverable and Definition-of-Done item mapped to the implementing task and file; deviations called out by name.\n- `## Coding-standard alignment` — per-file pass/fail against `coding.md` hard limits; any over-limit files flagged with the required justification.\n- `## Excluded-context auditability` — audit of `ExcludedMemory.exclusion_reason` population across all drop/skip paths, MCP `include_excluded` behaviour, and event-payload diffs containing inclusion/exclusion reasons.\n- `## Findings` — concrete observations, including verification-command output excerpts and any issues uncovered.\n- `## Verdict` — `Pass`, `Pass with follow-ups`, or `Fail with required rework`; if `Fail`, the report names the tasks that must be reopened before Phase 6 work can start.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md`\n- `grep -q '## Spec alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md`\n- `grep -q '## Excluded-context auditability' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md`\n- `grep -q '## Verdict' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R38-working-memory.md`\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- The review report file ≤800 lines.\n- Function/nesting/cyclomatic limits not applicable (docs task).\n- Schema parity: not applicable directly, but the review confirms T34's schema parity between `working_memory_checkpoints` columns and `WorkingMemoryState` fields.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, or commented-out code in the report.\n- No broken windows: every follow-up is filed with a target task ID, never left as \"we'll come back to this.\"\n- Doc citation: the report cites the exact spec headings reviewed, per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` \"Markdown Heading References\".\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review report exists at the expected path with all four required headings.\n- [ ] Every verification command from T34–T37 was run and its result recorded.\n- [ ] Eleven spec-named state fields confirmed present.\n- [ ] Eight required operations confirmed present and contract-tested.\n- [ ] Excluded-context auditability audit covers all drop/skip paths, the MCP tool's `include_excluded` mode, and event-payload diffs.\n- [ ] Checkpoint round-trip byte-identical and version-rejection behaviour confirmed.\n- [ ] Phase 4 forward-compatibility check confirms `RetrievalBundle` shape was preserved.\n- [ ] Verdict is recorded; if not `Pass`, follow-ups name specific tasks to reopen.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every spec §Phase 5 Definition-of-Done item is asserted in the report.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3027,"cache_read_input_tokens":82079,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3027,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6e3f0850-0c5a-4677-99ad-a673470bd600","request_id":"req_011Cb7NQb1jiUJr7CRQTKm9E","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2056:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2711:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:631:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Pnh5gggscokdtYCqGBG6x","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# T02 — Phase 0 — Successor architecture overview + compatibility policy\n2\t\n3\t**Phase:** 0\n4\t**Type:** docs\n5\t**Model class:** balanced\n6\t**Depends on:** T01\n7\t**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 0: Fork Foundation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation)\n8\t**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n9\t\n10\t## Goal\n11\t\n12\tThis task produces the two foundational design documents that govern every later phase: the successor architecture overview (workspace graph, event log, memory graph as three first-class sources of truth) and the MCP compatibility policy (which existing tools remain stable, which gain additive fields, which are deprecated with shims). It matters because spec §Design Thesis requires all three substrates to share identity, storage, and ranking primitives — without a single overview doc, each phase risks diverging. The compatibility policy is binding for every Phase 8 tool redesign and protects existing assistant clients while the substrate evolves.\n13\t\n14\t## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n15\t\n16\t> \"The correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth: 1. Workspace graph: files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries. 2. Event log: every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome. 3. Memory graph: durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\"\n17\t\n18\t> \"Phase 0 Deliverables: ... architecture docs copied and revised ... compatibility policy for existing MCP tools.\"\n19\t\n20\t## Inputs\n21\t\n22\t- Dependency outputs: `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md` from T01 (decides the branch/repo target for these docs).\n23\t- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n24\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n25\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n26\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n27\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n28\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n29\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` (current 43 registered tools)\n30\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` (current workflow tools)\n31\t- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth, citation discipline); `AGENTS.md` \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\".\n32\t\n33\t## Steps\n34\t\n35\t1. Read T01's decision doc and the spec sections §Design Thesis, §System Architecture, §MCP Surface, §MCP Tool Contract Principles.\n36\t2. In `2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md`, write a `## Workspace graph` section enumerating the node and edge families from spec §Workspace Graph and mapping each to the existing or planned crate.\n37\t3. Write a `## Event log` section summarizing the 20 event types, the append-only invariant, compaction snapshots, and the hot-path budget (≤5ms P99) from spec §Event Log and §Phase 2 DoD.\n38\t4. Write a `## Memory graph` section covering the memory classes, link types, evidence model, and the `CounterMemory` distinction from spec §Memory Graph.\n39\t5. Add a `## Shared substrate primitives` section explaining how identity, storage, and ranking are shared across the three substrates.\n40\t6. Add a `## Reference to existing docs` section linking forward to the prior `2026-04-11-*` architecture docs without duplicating their content.\n41\t7. In `2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`, enumerate every existing MCP tool by name and assign each a status: `stable` (no breaking change), `additive` (gains optional fields only), `redesigned-with-shim` (legacy name keeps responding with a compatibility shim), or `deprecated-with-deadline`.\n42\t8. Write a `## Backward compatibility` section stating the binding rule: every existing assistant client must continue to function for a full phase cycle after any tool's redesign lands. Include the shim removal protocol.\n43\t9. Cross-link the two docs and reference the spec heading anchors precisely.\n44\t\n45\t## Files expected\n46\t\n47\t- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — high-level architecture overview anchoring the three substrates and shared primitives for every later phase.\n48\t- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — per-tool stability classification and the backward-compatibility rule that binds Phase 8 redesign.\n49\t\n50\t## Verification\n51\t\n52\t- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the architecture doc exists.\n53\t- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — confirms the compatibility policy exists.\n54\t- `grep -q '## Workspace graph' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the workspace graph substrate is documented under its own heading.\n55\t- `grep -q '## Event log' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the event log substrate is documented under its own heading.\n56\t- `grep -q '## Memory graph' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the memory graph substrate is documented under its own heading.\n57\t- `grep -q '## Backward compatibility' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — confirms the backward-compatibility rule is stated under a dedicated heading rather than implied.\n58\t\n59\t## Coding-standard checks\n60\t\n61\t- File length ≤800 lines per file (verify with `wc -l docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`).\n62\t- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — not applicable (docs task).\n63\t- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n64\t- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n65\t- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming — applies to any pseudo-signatures shown in the docs.\n66\t- For schema changes: not applicable (no schema changes in this task).\n67\t- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Design Thesis`, `## System Architecture`, and `## MCP Surface` headings from the spec; reference `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-*.md` headings rather than duplicating content (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n68\t\n69\t## Definition of done\n70\t\n71\t- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n72\t- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n73\t- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n74\t- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 0 punted to a later phase.\n75\t- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"8176b57f-8fee-494d-b55e-cffaa1f6f7f0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:34:18.997Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2059:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2715:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:642:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01XTcDiMrgdjkzP2h53pbga2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SUPWwtG9FxhbV4ptXmemxT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T56.md","content":"# T56 — Phase 8 — Redesigned prepare_change + plan_edit + trace_scenario + diagnose_failure\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-8-workflow-engine-v2)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task redesigns the four edit-planning workflow tools (`prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `trace_scenario`, `diagnose_failure`) on top of the Phase 1–7 substrates: unified identity, event log, memory graph, retrieval pipeline, working memory, consolidation proposals, and verification status. The redesigned tools must compose those substrates in one call so that an assistant gets a complete edit-planning bundle (code anchors, docs, tests, memory with inclusion reasons, risks, verification commands) without first issuing dozens of low-level discovery calls. This is the central deliverable of Phase 8: spec §Phase 8 DoD requires \"common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\" and \"workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping.\" Without this redesign, the new substrate work in Phases 1–7 stays invisible to callers.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 8 Deliverables: redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation; workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates; one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands; workflow outcome recording integrated by default.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls; workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping.\"\n\n> \"Every assistant-facing tool should return: overview, ranked pivots, relevant context, memory highlights, event episodes where relevant, suggested next expansion, stable handles, risks or uncertainty, compact/full render choice, structured payload.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — verification statuses (`verified` | `unverified` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) must be surfaced inside the bundle so stale memory cannot appear as trusted guidance per spec §Phase 7 DoD.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/prepare_change.rs` — current `prepare_change` heuristics, retained as a compatibility shim per the T02 compatibility policy.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/plan_edit.rs` — current patch-oriented planning.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/trace_scenario.rs` — current scenario tracer.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/diagnose_failure.rs` — current failure-diagnosis heuristics.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — current MCP tool registration; new `workflow_v2` tools register alongside legacy names.\n  - Phase 1 identity types (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `EventId`, `MemoryId`, `ContextHandleId`).\n  - Phase 2 event writer (`EventWriter`) — every workflow call must emit `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, and `PlanCreated` events.\n  - Phase 4 retrieval pipeline (intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidate retrieval, scoring with inclusion reasons).\n  - Phase 5 working-memory operations (`retrieve`, `summarize`, `filter`, `pin`, `evict`, `expand`, `compress`, `checkpoint`).\n  - Phase 7 verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry checks).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references for spec-bound behavior; `AGENTS.md` \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" — legacy tool names continue to respond for a full phase cycle.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 8 and §MCP Tool Contract Principles in full. Copy the bundle field list (\"overview, ranked pivots, relevant context, memory highlights, event episodes, suggested next expansion, stable handles, risks, render choice, structured payload\") into a doc comment on a shared `WorkflowBundle` struct so the schema is self-documenting.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/mod.rs` exporting the four redesigned tools and the shared bundle types (`WorkflowBundle`, `Pivot`, `MemoryHighlight`, `EventEpisode`, `RiskNote`, `ExpansionHint`). Doc-comment the module with a reference to spec §Phase 8.\n3. Implement `prepare_change` in `workflow_v2/prepare_change.rs`: classify intent via the Phase 4 classifier, resolve anchors (paths, symbols, errors, APIs, config keys) into stable identities, run hybrid candidate retrieval across graph + docs + memory + events, apply the Phase 4 scorer, then assemble a `WorkflowBundle` containing code anchors, docs sections, candidate test files (from Phase 4 ranking + `tested_by` edges), memory highlights (each carrying inclusion reason + verification status per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles), risk notes derived from contradiction/supersession state, and verification commands (`cargo test ...`, `npm test`, etc.) inferred from the affected build targets.\n4. Implement `plan_edit` in `workflow_v2/plan_edit.rs`: build on `prepare_change` output and produce a patch-oriented plan with ordered edit steps, each step referencing files/symbols by stable identity, named tests to add or run, docs sections to update (so spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles \"every workflow tool should record inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded high-scoring candidates\" is satisfied).\n5. Implement `trace_scenario` in `workflow_v2/trace_scenario.rs`: walk call chains using the Phase 1 graph between anchor symbols, attaching event-episode evidence where the chain has been exercised before (from event log) and memory annotations describing prior successes/failures on that chain.\n6. Implement `diagnose_failure` in `workflow_v2/diagnose_failure.rs`: take a stack trace, compiler error, or failing test as input, extract anchors, query `FailurePattern` memories and prior `WorkflowFailed` events for similar signatures, surface likely culprit symbols with inclusion reasons + verification status, and suggest a candidate `prepare_change` follow-up bundle handle.\n7. Every tool must emit `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, and `PlanCreated` events via the Phase 2 `EventWriter`. Hot-path budget per spec §Phase 2 DoD: event write must not add more than 5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule`.\n8. Every tool must produce compact rendering by default with stable expansion handles (per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles \"compact rendering, full structured JSON, context handles, stable expansion targets, budget controls, diagnostic explanations where useful\"). The compact form must include enough inclusion reason for an assistant to trust the result.\n9. Register the four `workflow_v2` tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` under their canonical names while keeping the legacy implementations registered with deprecation flags per the T02 compatibility policy.\n10. In `workflow_v2/edit_workflows_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert that `prepare_change` returns a `WorkflowBundle` with at least one ranked pivot, one memory highlight (or an explicit empty rationale), one risk note, and at least one verification command for a fixture repo; (c) assert that no memory with `verification_status = stale | contradicted | superseded | expired | invalidated` appears without an explicit stale label (spec §Phase 7 DoD); (d) assert event-emission order and hot-path budget; (e) assert `plan_edit` produces ordered steps each referencing stable identity types; (f) assert `diagnose_failure` recovers an injected fixture failure pattern.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/prepare_change.rs` — redesigned `prepare_change` composing graph + event + memory + working-memory substrates into a one-call edit-planning bundle per spec §Phase 8.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/plan_edit.rs` — redesigned `plan_edit` producing ordered patch steps with stable-identity anchors, tests, docs updates, and verification commands.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/trace_scenario.rs` — redesigned `trace_scenario` walking call chains and attaching event/memory annotations.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/diagnose_failure.rs` — redesigned `diagnose_failure` matching failures against `FailurePattern` memory and prior `WorkflowFailed` events.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/edit_workflows_tests.rs` — unit + golden tests for each redesigned tool covering schema, stale-label discipline, event emission, hot-path budget, and recovery of fixture failure patterns.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/prepare_change.rs` — confirms the redesigned `prepare_change` file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/plan_edit.rs` — confirms the redesigned `plan_edit` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release` — the new workflow_v2 module compiles cleanly with the rest of the daemon.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::workflow_v2::edit_workflows_tests` — every edit-workflow test passes (schema, stale-label discipline, event emission, hot-path budget, failure-pattern recovery).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split per-tool when needed; do not co-locate two redesigned tools in one file).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `WorkflowBundle` and per-tool request/response structs derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone` and every public field has a doc comment.\n- No silent error handling — every retrieval/verifier/event-writer failure returns a typed error and surfaces in the bundle as a `RiskNote` rather than being swallowed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (e.g. `MemoryHighlight::inclusion_reason`, not `MemoryHighlight::why_str`).\n- For SQLite: not applicable directly; storage access goes through Phase 2 event writer and Phase 3 memory store APIs, never raw SQL in this module.\n- Observability: every tool emits at least one `tracing::info!` span with `tool`, `task_id`, `session_id`, `bundle_size_bytes`, `event_count` fields per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2` and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments where behavior is bound by those sections.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every redesigned tool composes graph + event + memory + working-memory substrates in one call; no follow-up \"polish\" task is permitted to add the substrate composition later.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; legacy `prepare_change` / `plan_edit` / `trace_scenario` / `diagnose_failure` continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3831,"cache_read_input_tokens":55400,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3831,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"05c46d50-b298-4f29-a42d-2dbcb0968787","request_id":"req_011Cb7MmdS4mZnDvp53Qjt6x","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2061:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2717:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:651:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RsNa6ebsnWVeUhpsNvGQen","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T06.md","content":"# T06 — Phase 1 — Identity type system (File, Symbol, Doc, Section, Event, Memory, Handle)\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R05\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified identity type system that every later substrate (event log, memory graph, working memory, retrieval, verification) depends on. Spec §Phase 1 states \"Identity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation.\" The task delivers stable, encodable, decodable, hashable id types for each of the seven identity kinds plus the encoding/decoding rules that make them compact in MCP payloads. The existing `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` is generalized into a common identity model.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles; identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs; explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads; compatibility support for legacy name-based references; tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity; ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches; identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R05 review (validates Phase 0 artifacts) and the layout decisions in `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-crate-boundary-plan.md` (decides whether identity is a `lattice-identity` crate or a `lattice-core::identity` module).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (current node identifier types)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` (current `SymbolId` usage in `expand_context`)\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` (existing handle conventions to honor and generalize)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), naming (intent over mechanism), single source of truth (replace ad-hoc id strings, do not fork them); `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility (legacy `SymbolId` callers must keep compiling via a re-export or `From` impl).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read the crate boundary plan from T03 to confirm whether identity lives in `daemon/crates/lattice-identity/` or `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/`. The files_expected list assumes the in-`lattice-core` module layout; if T03 chose a separate crate, mirror the file structure under that crate and update the workspace `Cargo.toml`.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` declaring the `kinds`, `encoding`, and `tests` submodules and re-exporting the public identity types.\n3. In `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs`, define one struct per identity kind with the exact fields required by the spec node/event/memory model:\n   - `FileId { workspace_id, repo_relative_path, content_hash }`\n   - `SymbolId { file: FileId, qualified_name, byte_offset, kind }` (generalizing the existing `SymbolId`)\n   - `DocId { workspace_id, repo_relative_path, content_hash }`\n   - `SectionId { doc: DocId, heading_path: Vec<String>, byte_offset }`\n   - `EventId { workspace_id, ulid }` (ULID for monotonic ordering)\n   - `MemoryId { workspace_id, ulid }`\n   - `ContextHandleId { workspace_id, session_id, ulid }`\n4. Each id type derives `Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize`. Implement `Display` to produce the compact MCP wire form.\n5. Define a common `IdentityKind` enum and an `Identity` enum that wraps the seven kinds so any subsystem can hold a heterogeneous identity reference.\n6. In `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/encoding.rs`, implement compact wire encoding rules: a stable string form (`file:<workspace>/<path>@<hash>`, `symbol:<file>#<qname>@<offset>`, `event:<workspace>/<ulid>`, etc.) and a decoder that round-trips. Reject malformed strings with a typed `IdentityDecodeError`.\n7. Provide `From<old_SymbolId> for SymbolId` and a re-export under the prior path so existing callers continue compiling.\n8. In `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/tests.rs`, write unit tests for: round-trip encoding for every kind; equality semantics; hash stability across process restarts (deterministic for same inputs); `IdentityDecodeError` cases (empty, missing prefix, malformed hash); `From<old_SymbolId>` migration.\n9. Add the module to `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` with `pub mod identity;` and document the substrate in a module-level doc comment that cites the spec heading.\n10. Run `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` and `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::tests` to confirm green.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` — module entrypoint, re-exports, module docs citing spec §Phase 1.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs` — the seven id structs, `Identity` enum, `IdentityKind` enum.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/encoding.rs` — compact wire form encode/decode with `IdentityDecodeError`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/tests.rs` — unit tests covering round-trip, equality, hashing, decode errors, and the legacy `SymbolId` migration shim.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` — confirms the module file exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs` — confirms the kinds file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` — confirms the crate compiles with the new module integrated.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::tests` — confirms all identity unit tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/*.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — applies to every encode/decode helper.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions; if `#[allow(clippy::...)]` is required, justify on the same line.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `IdentityDecodeError`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable (no SQL schema in this task); type-level schema parity is asserted via the `Identity` enum covering all seven kinds with no orphan fields.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec in `mod.rs`; reference `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` headings rather than duplicating prior handle behavior (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"36cefa05-6830-4681-aa11-def156e8cd29","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2062:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2718:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:660:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VyFsJMQCiudocXkZ3sZYhd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T07.md","content":"# T07 — Phase 1 — Identity resolver with ambiguity diagnostics\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T06\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task implements the resolver that turns user-supplied or LLM-supplied references — paths, qualified symbol names, doc headings, test names, event refs — into the stable `Identity` values defined in T06. Spec §Phase 1 names \"identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\" as a deliverable and binds the resolver to return ambiguity diagnostics rather than arbitrary matches when an input has multiple candidate identities. Without this resolver, every later phase would re-implement name→id lookups inconsistently, and ambiguity would be silently swallowed.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: ... identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs ... compatibility support for legacy name-based references.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity; ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: identity types from T06 (`daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs`, `encoding.rs`).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (workspace graph that backs path/symbol lookups)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` (SQLite-backed lookups for files, symbols, docs, sections)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/parser/` (doc heading and symbol extraction the resolver consumes)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` hard limits, naming (intent over mechanism), error handling (resolver errors must be structured, never silent); `AGENTS.md` (resolver public API is binding once shipped — any change is a contract change).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs` with a `pub struct IdentityResolver` that holds references to the graph, storage, and parser indexes needed to answer lookups.\n2. Define the resolver public API:\n   - `resolve_path(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, path: &str) -> Result<FileId, ResolveError>`\n   - `resolve_symbol(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, qualified_name: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SymbolId>`\n   - `resolve_section(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, doc: &DocId, heading: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SectionId>`\n   - `resolve_test(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, test_name: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SymbolId>` (tests are identified as symbols with a `Test` kind)\n   - `resolve_event_ref(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, ref_str: &str) -> Result<EventId, ResolveError>`\n3. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/ambiguity.rs` with the `ResolveOutcome<T>` enum (`Unique(T)`, `Ambiguous(AmbiguityReport<T>)`, `NotFound(ResolveError)`) and the `AmbiguityReport<T>` struct carrying the list of candidates plus a `disambiguation_hint` describing how the caller can narrow the query (suggest adding parent module, file path, byte offset, etc.).\n4. The resolver must never pick arbitrarily when multiple matches exist — return `Ambiguous`. Tests in T09 will assert this on duplicate symbol names.\n5. Add a legacy compatibility helper `pub fn resolve_legacy_symbol_name(&self, name: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SymbolId>` that takes a name without workspace context and routes to the resolver via the default workspace, preserving older MCP payloads.\n6. Implement caching: keep a small LRU keyed by `(workspace, query)` so the same resolution within a session does not re-traverse the graph. Cache hits must still preserve ambiguity semantics — a cached `Ambiguous` returns `Ambiguous`.\n7. Errors are structured: `ResolveError::WorkspaceNotIndexed`, `ResolveError::Malformed`, `ResolveError::IndexLagBehind { latest_event_id }`. No bare `String` errors.\n8. Add module-level docs in both files citing spec §Phase 1.\n9. Register the new submodules in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` with `pub mod resolver;` and `pub mod ambiguity;`.\n10. Write smoke unit tests inline (the full ambiguity / rename / branch-change test matrix is owned by T09): construct a tiny resolver fixture and assert `resolve_path` returns a `FileId`, `resolve_symbol` on a duplicate name returns `Ambiguous`, and `resolve_legacy_symbol_name` falls back correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs` — `IdentityResolver` struct, the five `resolve_*` methods, LRU cache, structured `ResolveError`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/ambiguity.rs` — `ResolveOutcome<T>`, `AmbiguityReport<T>`, `disambiguation_hint` helpers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs` — confirms the resolver file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` — confirms the crate compiles with the resolver and ambiguity modules wired into `lattice-core`.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::resolver` — confirms the resolver smoke tests pass.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::ambiguity` — confirms the ambiguity module tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/ambiguity.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — every `resolve_*` method must fit; extract candidate-collection helpers if needed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`resolve_symbol` not `lookup`, `AmbiguityReport` not `MultiResult`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable (resolver consumes existing storage).\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec in module docs; if any non-obvious caching or LRU choice is made, cite the heading from `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` that authorizes the behavior (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1a20f582-7bfb-4d3c-8dba-826aa42e26d0","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2065:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2721:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:672:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016wpFR2N3dUb7fdjiDggmL3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T08.md","content":"# T08 — Phase 1 — Identity serialization in MCP payloads + legacy name compatibility shim\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T07\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task wires the new `Identity` types into the MCP JSON-RPC surface so every workflow tool response carries stable identities the client can expand later, while preserving legacy name-based fields so existing clients continue to function. Spec §Phase 1 deliverables include \"explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\" and \"compatibility support for legacy name-based references\". The compatibility shim is the binding mechanism by which T02's MCP compatibility policy is honored for Phase 1.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: ... explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads; compatibility support for legacy name-based references.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T06 identity types (`daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs`, `encoding.rs`).\n  - T07 resolver and ambiguity modules (`resolver.rs`, `ambiguity.rs`).\n  - T02 compatibility policy (`docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`) — binding for which tools must add additive identity fields vs. shimmed legacy fields.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` (current MCP dispatch and payload shapes)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` (JSON-RPC server)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` (handle cache that must store new `ContextHandleId`)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth, schema parity), `AGENTS.md` (binding compatibility shim — every legacy field stays populated for one phase cycle).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` with serde helpers that emit the compact wire form defined in T06 `encoding.rs` and accept either compact form or structured object form on deserialize.\n2. Define a `pub struct IdentityPayload` containing both the compact `id` string and a structured `fields` object, plus an optional `legacy_name` field for backward compatibility. Serialization round-trips through both forms.\n3. Add `pub fn serialize_outcome<T: Into<Identity>>(outcome: ResolveOutcome<T>) -> IdentityPayloadOrAmbiguity` that converts the resolver outcome to the wire shape, preserving `Ambiguous` as a structured diagnostic instead of an arbitrary pick.\n4. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` with the MCP wiring: a wrapper that decorates every workflow tool response (or its top-level pivot list) with `IdentityPayload` values and preserves the legacy name field listed in the T02 compatibility policy for each tool.\n5. Wire one representative tool (`get_context_capsule` or `prepare_change`) end-to-end through the new serialization wrapper as a proof of integration; full Phase 8 redesign of every tool is owned later, but Phase 1 must demonstrate the integration path works without regressing existing clients.\n6. Add tests under `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests`) covering: compact-form round-trip, structured-form acceptance, legacy-name preservation, ambiguity payload shape.\n7. Add tests under `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` covering: a full tool response that includes both identity payloads and legacy fields, and a response where the resolver returned `Ambiguous` (the response must surface the ambiguity diagnostic rather than silently picking).\n8. Update the workflow tool that was wired in step 5 to emit identity payloads alongside its prior fields; document the change in `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` under the per-tool classification.\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release`, `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- identity::serialization`, and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- rpc::identity_payload` to confirm green.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` — `IdentityPayload`, serde helpers, `serialize_outcome`, inline tests.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` — MCP wrapper that decorates workflow responses with identity payloads and preserves legacy name fields.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` — confirms the serialization module exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` — confirms the RPC integration module exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release` — confirms the full workspace builds in release profile with the new modules wired in.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- identity::serialization` — confirms the serialization tests pass.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- rpc::identity_payload` — confirms the RPC integration tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — applies to every serde helper and wrapper.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`IdentityPayload`, `serialize_outcome`, not `IdData` or `to_json`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable (no SQL schema added); JSON schema parity is asserted by tests covering both legacy and new fields.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec and the per-tool classification heading from `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` for every additive field introduced (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"f2172cba-383d-413f-b510-a7ed9826dfd5","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2066:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2722:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:684:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012ZX7F3AyQWmYbR3Eht2XW9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VnrPpS4EXKui27kxGWfrbU","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T30.md","content":"# T30 — Phase 4 — Scoring model with diagnostic mode (all required signals)\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T29\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Ranking Complexity](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#ranking-complexity)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers pipeline step 8 — the scorer — and the diagnostic mode that makes the scorer auditable per spec §Ranking Complexity. The scorer must apply every ranking signal the spec enumerates, expose each signal as a named, inspectable feature, and emit both scores and human-readable reasons under a diagnostic mode. Compact mode emits only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result; diagnostic mode emits the full per-signal breakdown. The scorer must remain feature-based — no learned policy is acceptable in Phase 4 — so retrieval ranking stays explainable for the lifetime of this phase.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Ranking signals: task-type compatibility; graph proximity to anchors; exact identifier match; semantic similarity; verification status; freshness; scope; evidence strength; contradiction/supersession state; past usefulness; recent successful reuse; user preference compatibility; token cost.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness ... diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons ...\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"Risk: ranking becomes hard to reason about. Control: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\" (spec §Ranking Complexity)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T29 `Candidate` stream — provides candidates with source, seed anchor, traversal path, and preliminary inclusion reason.\n  - T27 `IntentClassification` — drives `task-type compatibility`.\n  - T28 `ResolvedAnchor` set — drives `exact identifier match` and `graph proximity to anchors`.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — provides `verification status`, `freshness`, `scope`, `evidence strength`, `contradiction/supersession state` fields per the Phase 3 schema.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` (Phase 2) — provides `past usefulness` and `recent successful reuse` via memory-access events and `WorkflowSucceeded`/`MemoryRetrieved`.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/embeddings/` — provides `semantic similarity` (cosine) for the candidate's text against the anchor/intent text.\n  - User-preference store from Phase 3 (memory of `UserPreferenceObserved`) — drives `user preference compatibility`.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, table-driven scoring); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references in code comments where behaviour is doc-anchored).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Retrieval Engine \"Ranking signals\" and §Ranking Complexity in full; copy the thirteen-signal list verbatim into a doc-comment table at the top of `scoring.rs`.\n2. Define the data structures: `SignalScore { signal: SignalKind, raw: f32, weighted: f32, reason: String }`, `RankedCandidate { candidate: Candidate, total_score: f32, signal_scores: Vec<SignalScore>, inclusion_reason: String }`, `ScoringWeights` (one weight per signal, defaulting from a documented `RetrievalProfile`), and `DiagnosticMode { Compact, Diagnostic }`.\n3. Implement one pure-function evaluator per signal in `scoring.rs`, each ≤50 lines, each returning a `SignalScore` with both score and reason. The thirteen evaluators are: `task_type_compatibility`, `graph_proximity`, `exact_identifier_match`, `semantic_similarity`, `verification_status`, `freshness`, `scope`, `evidence_strength`, `contradiction_or_supersession_state`, `past_usefulness`, `recent_successful_reuse`, `user_preference_compatibility`, `token_cost`. Each MUST be present; missing data yields a score of 0 and a reason like `\"no <field> available; signal skipped\"`, never a panic or silent skip.\n4. Implement `score_candidates(candidates: Vec<Candidate>, intent: &IntentClassification, anchors: &[ResolvedAnchor], weights: &ScoringWeights, mode: DiagnosticMode, ctx: &ScoringContext) -> Vec<RankedCandidate>` that calls each evaluator, sums weighted scores, fills `inclusion_reason` with a compact summary in `Compact` mode and the full per-signal list in `Diagnostic` mode, then sorts descending.\n5. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs`: the public API for diagnostic mode. Exposes `RankingDiagnostics { intent, anchors, candidates_per_source, ranked: Vec<RankedCandidate>, weights, budget_exhaustion_flags }` plus serde derivation, so T31 (shaper) and T36 (MCP working-memory inspector) can hand the diagnostics straight to the caller without further transformation.\n6. Honor spec §Stale Memory Leakage by ensuring `verification_status` and `contradiction_or_supersession_state` apply a hard penalty (not just a soft weight) when status is `stale`, `contradicted`, or `superseded`, so these candidates cannot rank above trusted ones except when diagnostics explicitly request them. The penalty value and policy live in `ScoringWeights` with inline documentation citing `## Stale Memory Leakage`.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring_tests.rs` covering: each of the thirteen signals is evaluated for a representative candidate; signal absence yields a `0.0` score and a documented reason (no panic); stale/contradicted candidates rank below trusted candidates with the same raw match; diagnostic mode round-trips signal scores and reasons through serde; compact mode produces a non-empty single-line inclusion reason; weight overrides change the ranking deterministically; user-preference compatibility raises the score of candidates matching a stored preference and lowers candidates conflicting with one.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring.rs` — signal evaluators, weights, orchestrator.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs` — diagnostics types and serde, public API for diagnostic mode.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for every signal, both modes, and the stale-memory hard penalty.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring.rs` — confirms the scorer module exists at the expected path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs` — confirms the diagnostics module exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::scoring_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every signal and both modes are covered.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. If `scoring.rs` approaches the cap, split signal evaluators into `scoring/` submodules grouped by data dependency (graph signals, memory signals, history signals).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Each signal evaluator is its own function.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task, but field reads from `memories` use the canonical structs).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: weights, defaults, and signal-kind enum are defined once and consumed everywhere; no parallel weight tables in callers.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; no silent `.unwrap()` on Optional signal data.\n- Doc citation: the signal table at the top of `scoring.rs` cites `## 7. Retrieval Engine`; the stale-memory hard penalty comment cites `## Stale Memory Leakage` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the thirteen ranking signals from spec §Retrieval Engine is implemented as a signal evaluator with a test.\n- [ ] Diagnostic mode emits both per-signal scores and reasons; compact mode emits a single-line inclusion reason — both verified by tests.\n- [ ] Stale/contradicted/superseded candidates cannot rank above trusted candidates with the same raw match (regression test pinned).\n- [ ] Diagnostics struct is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T31 (shaper) and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3306,"cache_read_input_tokens":52740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3306,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":58,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"51d549ac-52ac-4ed7-9cd1-26fc808d3401","request_id":"req_011Cb7MvMPAgod1NwX41bo3F","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2070:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2726:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:711:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016MBNff4Vmm8f1myrXiWNYC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WwRjPQsgfzTC4J4X1QNmPX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T59.md","content":"# T59 — Phase 8 — New memory tools: verify_memory + explain_memory (unified) + list_memory_conflicts\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified `verify_explain_memory` MCP tool (collapsing the spec's `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` per §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\") and the new `list_memory_conflicts` tool. The unified tool runs verification checks against a memory and returns both the resulting status (`verified` | `unverified` | `in_review` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) and a structured explanation of why each linked file/symbol/doc/test contributed to that status. `list_memory_conflicts` surfaces all contradiction and supersession edges anchored on a target memory or graph node so an operator can review them. Both tools are required by spec §MCP Surface and are the trust surface for the verification engine — without them, the Phase 7 verifier produces statuses that no assistant or operator can interrogate.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: …`verify_memory`, `explain_memory`, `list_memory_conflicts`…\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: …audit whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Verification outputs: `verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`.\" (spec §Verification Engine)\n\n> \"Every memory-bearing response should identify: why the memory was included; evidence strength; verification status; freshness status; contradiction/supersession state; scope; expansion target.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — `verify_explain_memory` is the MCP surface for the Phase 7 verifier; its outputs must match the Phase 7 status taxonomy exactly.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verifier (existence, span, scope, expiry checks; incremental verification jobs).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory store + links + evidence + accesses + scores.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer (emits `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryInvalidated`).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - Phase 1 identity types.\n  - T58 shared `MemoryRecord` type.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface and §Verification Engine in full. Quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` so the rationale for unifying `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` is preserved at the call site.\n2. Implement `verify_explain_memory` in `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` as the unified tool. Accept `memory_id: MemoryId` plus optional `mode: \"verify\" | \"explain\" | \"verify_and_explain\"` (default `verify_and_explain`). Internally run the Phase 7 verifier on the memory, then construct an `ExplainReport` that itemizes each verification check (linked file exists, linked symbol exists, cited doc exists, linked test exists, evidence span still matches, contradiction state coherent, scope leak absent, time-bound expiry honored) and the outcome per check. Return a `VerifyExplainResponse` with `status: VerificationStatus`, `checks: Vec<CheckResult>` (each carrying `kind`, `target`, `outcome`, `evidence_ref`), `confidence_delta`, and an `expansion_handle` so the caller can pull richer context.\n3. Mode semantics: `verify` returns only the status + a minimal summary; `explain` skips re-running checks and returns the last persisted `ExplainReport`; `verify_and_explain` runs verification and returns the freshly-derived `ExplainReport`. Default is `verify_and_explain` because spec §Verification Engine and §MCP Tool Contract Principles together require fresh, explainable status on every memory surface.\n4. Implement `list_memory_conflicts` in `memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs`. Accept `anchor: MemoryId | FileId | SymbolId | DocSectionId`, traverse `contradicts` and `supersedes` link types from Phase 3 memory_links, and return a paginated list of `ConflictRecord`s each carrying `source`, `target`, `link_type` (`contradicts` | `supersedes`), `link_strength`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `link_verification_status`, and `reason`. Honor scope filters per spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage.\"\n5. Both tools must emit appropriate Phase 2 events: `verify_explain_memory` emits `MemoryRetrieved` plus `MemoryInvalidated` when status transitions away from `verified`; `list_memory_conflicts` emits `MemoryRetrieved` for each surfaced conflict.\n6. Both tools must support compact + full + diagnostic render modes per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles. Compact mode for `verify_explain_memory` returns status + one-line per failed check; diagnostic mode returns the full check trace.\n7. Register the two tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Per the T02 compatibility policy, the spec's nominal `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` names continue to respond as deprecation shims that forward to `verify_explain_memory` with the appropriate `mode`. The shims emit a deprecation warning in the response.\n8. In `memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert `verify_explain_memory` returns the exact Phase 7 status taxonomy and never invents a new status string; (c) assert every check result carries an evidence reference; (d) assert a memory whose linked symbol was deleted transitions to `stale` with a `linked_symbol_missing` check failure; (e) assert `list_memory_conflicts` returns both `contradicts` and `supersedes` edges with correct directionality; (f) assert scope-leak negative tests; (g) assert the `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond and forward correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — unified verify+explain tool with `mode` parameter, full check trace, and expansion handle, per spec §MCP Surface tool-surface discipline.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — `list_memory_conflicts` traversing contradiction + supersession links with scope-aware pagination.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs` — unit + scope-leak negative tests covering schema, status taxonomy parity with Phase 7, evidence-reference completeness, conflict directionality, and shim forwarding.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — confirms the unified verify+explain file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — confirms the `list_memory_conflicts` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::memory_v2::verify_explain_tests` — every verify/explain/conflicts test passes (schema, status parity, evidence completeness, conflict directionality, scope-leak negatives, shim forwarding).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `VerifyExplainResponse`, `CheckResult`, `ConflictRecord` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; the `mode` enum is serde-tagged with snake_case wire names; the `VerificationStatus` enum reuses the Phase 7 type rather than declaring a parallel one.\n- No silent error handling — every verifier failure returns a typed error; scope-leak rejections are explicit errors, not empty results.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`CheckResult::evidence_ref`, not `CheckResult::ref_str`).\n- For SQLite: all queries go through Phase 3 store and Phase 7 verifier APIs; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `memory_id`, `scope`, `status`, `check_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## MCP Surface`, `## Verification Engine`, and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the unified `verify_explain_memory` ships in this task; the spec's two-tool API is collapsed before MCP contract stabilization per §MCP Surface.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4764,"cache_read_input_tokens":68448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4764,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6925f9b8-658b-4628-9f96-5512a5babaa4","request_id":"req_011Cb7MzXVz1xvApfw62RZzy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2076:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2732:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:755:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_017cwZPiuMEuGQCXdSJKKNBF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JyKU6KBLzr4ubAFG9yoFws","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T34.md","content":"# T34 — Phase 5 — Working memory state model + working_memory_checkpoints table\n\n**Phase:** 5\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R33\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [§5. Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#5-working-memory), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the per-task, short-lived working-memory state model the spec mandates in §5 — making context management explicit instead of implicit prompt accumulation — and pins it to a versioned `working_memory_checkpoints` SQLite table so checkpoints can be restored. The state model contains exactly the eleven fields the spec enumerates; the checkpoint schema preserves the full state along with timestamps and a checkpoint name so T35's `checkpoint` operation has a target to write into and T37's restore tests have a target to restore from.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Working memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation. Working memory contains: task statement; interpreted intent; active files and symbols; active hypotheses; active failures; current plan; selected memories; excluded memories and reasons; budget decisions; unresolved questions; verification status.\" (spec §5. Working Memory)\n\n> \"Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: explicit per-task working memory state; operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint; MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context; tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - R33 verdict — Phase 4 retrieval is complete; `RetrievalBundle` / `BundleResult` are stable and can be embedded under `selected_memories`/`excluded_memories`.\n  - T27 `IntentClassification` — populates `interpreted_intent`.\n  - T28 `ResolvedAnchor` set — populates `active_files_and_symbols` seeds.\n  - T31 `RetrievalBundle` — supplies `selected_memories` and `excluded_memories` (with reasons) verbatim per the Phase 4 forward-compatibility contract.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — SQLite connection conventions, migration helpers.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — for the `verification_status` enum reused under \"verification status\".\n  - Phase 1 identity (`IdentityResolver`) — every reference inside working memory uses a stable identity.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, schema parity); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references; project build commands).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §5. Working Memory and §Phase 5: Working Memory in full; copy the eleven-field state list verbatim into a doc comment at the top of `state.rs`.\n2. Create the module skeleton: `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs` re-exports `state`, `operations` (T35), `event_hooks` (T36) and registers the module with `lib.rs`.\n3. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state.rs` with `WorkingMemoryState { task_statement: String, interpreted_intent: IntentClassification, active_files: BTreeSet<FileIdentity>, active_symbols: BTreeSet<SymbolIdentity>, active_hypotheses: Vec<Hypothesis>, active_failures: Vec<FailureRecord>, current_plan: Option<PlanRef>, selected_memories: Vec<BundleResult>, excluded_memories: Vec<ExcludedMemory>, budget_decisions: BudgetDecisions, unresolved_questions: Vec<String>, verification_status: WorkingMemoryVerification }`. Field names mirror the spec exactly. `ExcludedMemory { result: BundleResult, exclusion_reason: String }` makes the \"and reasons\" half of the spec phrase a type-system requirement, not a convention.\n4. Define supporting types in the same module: `Hypothesis { text, evidence_refs, confidence }`, `FailureRecord { kind, message, observed_at, evidence_refs }`, `PlanRef { plan_id, version }`, `BudgetDecisions { token_cap, dropped_count, truncated_results, pinned_identities }`, `WorkingMemoryVerification { last_verified_at, status: enum, notes }`.\n5. Author the schema SQL in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/schema.sql` for the `working_memory_checkpoints` table: `(checkpoint_id INTEGER PK, workspace_id TEXT NOT NULL, session_id TEXT NOT NULL, task_id TEXT NOT NULL, checkpoint_name TEXT NOT NULL, created_at INTEGER NOT NULL, state_version INTEGER NOT NULL, state_json TEXT NOT NULL, state_hash TEXT NOT NULL)` plus indexes on `(workspace_id, session_id, task_id, created_at DESC)` and `(state_hash)`. `state_version` is bumped whenever `WorkingMemoryState` shape changes; migrations are rejected at load time if `state_version` is unknown.\n6. Wire the migration into the existing storage bootstrap: `working_memory_checkpoints` lives in the existing memory DB (`.lattice/memories.db`) so it shares the session/task identity referenced elsewhere. Document this placement decision inline with `## Storage Design` citation.\n7. Implement `save_checkpoint(state: &WorkingMemoryState, name: &str, conn: &Connection) -> Result<CheckpointId>` and `load_checkpoint(id: CheckpointId, conn: &Connection) -> Result<WorkingMemoryState>` in `state.rs`. Serialization uses serde JSON; `state_hash` is a deterministic SHA-256 over the canonical-JSON form so restore can be verified bit-for-bit.\n8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state_tests.rs` covering: default-constructed state has the eleven required fields; serde round-trips the state without data loss; `save_checkpoint` followed by `load_checkpoint` returns a byte-identical `WorkingMemoryState`; `state_hash` matches between save and load; loading a checkpoint with an unknown `state_version` is rejected with a clear error; `excluded_memories` cannot be constructed without an `exclusion_reason` (type-system enforced).\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs` — module skeleton re-exporting state, operations, event hooks.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state.rs` — `WorkingMemoryState` + supporting types + save/load checkpoint API.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/schema.sql` — `working_memory_checkpoints` table DDL and indexes.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for shape, serde round-trip, checkpoint save/load, state-version rejection.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs`\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state.rs`\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib working_memory::state_tests`\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Save/load are each their own function; serialization helpers extracted if `state.rs` approaches the cap.\n- Schema parity: every column in `working_memory_checkpoints` has a corresponding `WorkingMemoryState` field or wrapper-record field; the `state_json` column mirrors the serde shape exactly. Schema doc and Rust struct are updated in the same commit.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: identity types are reused from Phase 1; no parallel `FileId` or `SymbolId` definitions.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; `ExcludedMemory.exclusion_reason` is non-optional.\n- Doc citation: the state-field table cites `## 5. Working Memory`; the checkpoint table placement cites `## Storage Design` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the eleven spec-named fields is in `WorkingMemoryState`; the `working_memory_checkpoints` table is created with versioned schema and deterministic hash.\n- [ ] `excluded_memories` carries a typed `exclusion_reason` — the \"and reasons\" spec requirement is enforced by the type system, not by convention.\n- [ ] Save/load round-trips a checkpoint byte-identically (regression test pinned).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3804,"cache_read_input_tokens":67149,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3804,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"34b5d4e7-9771-4353-b726-5d17e43a83af","request_id":"req_011Cb7NAia285c59JysE8vFy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2077:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2733:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:758:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019JjqMQ778TToFmau2PwXFQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TKbr7KNkKbifeCtRHe4Xvy","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md","content":"# T62 — Phase 8 — Workflow outcome recording integrated by default + composition tests\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T56, T57, T58, T59, T60, T61\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-8-workflow-engine-v2)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task wires automatic workflow-outcome recording into every redesigned tool from T56–T60 and ships the composition test suite that proves the new tools compose correctly over the underlying substrates. Spec §Phase 8 Deliverables names \"workflow outcome recording integrated by default\" as a binding deliverable. Spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles requires \"every workflow tool should record inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded high-scoring candidates where useful, response summary, downstream use events.\" Without this task, individual tools work in isolation but the event log + memory consolidation cannot observe whether a workflow succeeded, which breaks Phase 6 procedure extraction and Phase 9 success metrics.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 8 Deliverables: …workflow outcome recording integrated by default.\" (spec §Phase 8)\n\n> \"Every workflow tool should record: inputs; resolved anchors; selected candidates; excluded high-scoring candidates where useful; response summary; downstream use events.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n> \"Required event types: …`WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`…\" (spec §Event Log)\n\n> \"Definition of done: completed tasks produce useful episode memories; repeated successful traces produce procedural memories; consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered.\" (spec §Phase 6 — depends on outcome capture from this task)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T56 (`workflow_v2/` edit-planning tools).\n  - T57 (`workflow_v2/` discovery tools).\n  - T58–T60 (`memory_v2/` tools).\n  - T61 (final MCP tool reference).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer, including `WorkflowSucceeded` and `WorkflowFailed` envelopes.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/record_workflow_outcome.rs` — current explicit outcome recorder, retained as a manual override path.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/` — every tool from T56/T57 to be wrapped with the outcome interceptor.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/` — every tool from T58/T59/T60 to be wrapped with the outcome interceptor.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/session_metrics.rs` — outcome events feed session metrics.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability, §Tests as documentation; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 8, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, and §Event Log in full. Quote the \"Every workflow tool should record…\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` so the contract is enforced at the call site.\n2. Implement `workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` as a reusable interceptor: a `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` type that wraps any tool handler, captures inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded candidates (the top-N high-scoring candidates that were rejected and why), response summary, and emits a `WorkflowSucceeded` or `WorkflowFailed` event on completion. The recorder must run on the hot path within the spec §Phase 2 budget (≤5ms P99 overhead).\n3. Wire `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` into every redesigned tool from T56 and T57 by default. Wire it into every memory tool from T58/T59/T60 that participates in a user-facing workflow (excluding `get_event_trace`, which would create a feedback loop). Default-on means the tool registration in `mcp.rs` constructs the recorder around the handler; the legacy explicit `record_workflow_outcome` tool remains available as a manual override per the T02 compatibility policy.\n4. The recorder must also feed `session_metrics` so success rate, irrelevant-files-opened, and `workflow_success_after_first_plan` (from spec §Phase 9) are accumulated automatically.\n5. Define how \"downstream use events\" are tracked: when a returned context handle or memory ID is subsequently referenced in another tool call, the recorder back-links the later event to the originating workflow via the Phase 2 `EventEnvelope::references` field.\n6. In `workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs`, write composition tests that exercise the new tools end-to-end against a fixture workspace: (a) `prepare_change → plan_edit → apply_patch_simulated → record outcome` chain emits the correct sequence of events; (b) `get_context_capsule → expand_context` chain returns stable handle deltas without redundant payload; (c) `diagnose_failure → prepare_change` chain reuses anchors from the failure diagnosis; (d) `save_memory → verify_explain_memory` chain transitions an unverified memory to a verified state and emits the matching events; (e) `consolidate_session → propose_memory_evolution(action=\"apply\")` chain applies a proposal that the session produced; (f) every workflow chain emits exactly one `WorkflowSucceeded` or `WorkflowFailed` event per outer call; (g) excluded high-scoring candidates appear in the recorded outcome with rejection reasons.\n7. Assert that the hot-path budget is honored: composition tests measure the per-tool overhead added by `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` and fail if it exceeds the spec §Phase 2 ≤5ms P99 budget.\n8. Assert that scope-leak and workspace-boundary protections from T58–T60 are preserved when the recorder is in the path.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` — `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` interceptor capturing inputs, resolved anchors, selected/excluded candidates, response summary, and emitting `WorkflowSucceeded` / `WorkflowFailed` events per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles; wired by default into every T56–T60 tool.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs` — end-to-end composition tests covering chained tool calls, handle stability, anchor reuse, verification transitions, consolidation→apply chains, outcome emission, excluded-candidate recording, and hot-path budget.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` — confirms the recorder file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs` — confirms the composition test file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::workflow_v2::composition_tests` — every composition test passes (event ordering, handle stability, anchor reuse, verification transition, consolidation→apply, excluded-candidate recording, hot-path budget, scope-leak preservation).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: the recorded outcome payload uses the same `WorkflowSucceeded` / `WorkflowFailed` envelopes defined in Phase 2; no parallel outcome struct is introduced.\n- No silent error handling — recorder failures emit `tracing::warn!` AND surface as `RiskNote`s in the bundle, never swallowed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`WorkflowOutcomeRecorder::record_excluded`, not `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder::also_excl`).\n- For SQLite: outcome persistence goes through the Phase 2 event writer; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: the recorder emits one `tracing::info!` span per recorded outcome with `tool`, `task_id`, `session_id`, `outcome`, `excluded_count`, `latency_ms` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`, `## Event Log`, and `## Phase 2 — Event Log Substrate` Definition of Done (hot-path budget) in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the recorder is wired by default into every T56–T60 tool eligible for outcome capture; composition tests cover every chained scenario named in the steps; the hot-path budget assertion is live, not commented out.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the legacy explicit `record_workflow_outcome` tool continues to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3649,"cache_read_input_tokens":81273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":54,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"729bf2f0-fa94-4854-a179-85471920cbc3","request_id":"req_011Cb7NBPjimQ7hZ8gS7hpnH","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2080:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2736:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:782:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TwdU3puoByR54shmvCc2Fc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R78.md","content":"# R78 — Frontend review — Phase 10 human review UI (UI-spec compliance)\n\n**Phase:** Review for Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); definition-of-done checklist (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review certifies that the Phase 10 human review UI ships compliant with the shared UI specification, the Lattice coding standard, the spec's mandatory operator view set, and the i18n discipline that all user-facing strings live in a single catalog. It is the gate that R79 (contract gate) and R88 (definition-of-done gate) depend on: a frontend that bypasses `<dialog>`, swallows errors, or inlines English literals would force later phases into rework. Passing this review means the review surface is enterprise-grade on day one per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` (i18n helper)\n  - `extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/staleView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/review.test.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/runTest.ts`\n  - `extension/package.json` (review view + command contribution diffs)\n- Spec sections this phase claims to satisfy: `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (hard limits, error handling, single source of truth), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` (all sections — §3 accessibility, §6 lists & tables, §7 modals, §8 state management, §11 visual standards, §14 i18n, §16 telemetry), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`.\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Open every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm the eleven operator views from spec §10 are all reachable from `ReviewPanelProvider`: memory inbox, proposed promotions, proposed contradictions, stale memory list, memory evidence view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, usefulness metrics, workspace graph health, indexing health, consolidation queue. (Usefulness metrics may live in the existing sidebar from `sidebar.ts` if R70 confirmed that surface; either way the link must be reachable.)\n2. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for `window.confirm(`, `window.prompt(`, `window.alert(` — every hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §7. Grep for `<dialog` to confirm the modals from T73 and T75 use native `<dialog>` elements.\n3. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for inline English literals in JSX-equivalent strings: look for quoted strings inside webview HTML template literals that are not wrapped in `i18n.t(...)`. Any hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §14.\n4. Open `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` and confirm namespaces exist for: panel, statusBadge, memoryInbox, promotionQueue, contradictionQueue, proposalDialog, staleView, evidenceInspector, eventTraceView, retrievalExplanationView, consolidationQueueView, indexingHealthView, workspaceGraphHealthView. Spot-check that every column header, action label, empty state, and error state from the eleven views has a corresponding key.\n5. Open every mutation handler (accept/reject in `ProposalDialog`, re-verify in `staleView`/`evidenceInspector`, retry in `consolidationQueueView`, recompute in `workspaceGraphHealthView`) and confirm each is wrapped in `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` plus an inline error state. Silent `.catch(() => {})` or bare `await` without surrounding handler is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §8.5 and `coding.md` §Error handling.\n6. Confirm `StatusBadge.ts` covers all eight verification statuses from spec §8 (`verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`) and every additional badge family (proposal status, job status, decision badge in retrieval explanation). Confirm the file is the only place `verification-status → color/label` mapping lives.\n7. Open every table (memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace, candidate table in retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, per-family counts in graph health) and confirm sortable columns use the `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents exposed by T71 (per `ui-specification.md` §6.2). Confirm pagination is present on every list per `ui-specification.md` §6.1.\n8. Open every interactive surface and confirm it renders loading, empty, and error states explicitly (per `ui-specification.md` §8.2, §8.3, §8.5).\n9. Grep for `prompt(`, raw `<select>`, and `type=\"number\"` ID inputs — any hit for ID-bearing fields is a blocker. Confirm filter pickers use `vscode.window.showQuickPick`.\n10. Run `wc -l` on every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm no file exceeds 800 lines per `coding.md`.\n11. Grep all files for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` without justification — each hit is a blocker per `coding.md` §No broken windows.\n12. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint && npm test` and confirm everything passes.\n13. Cross-check that the smoke tests from T77 cover every subview from T72–T76 (activation, view registration, mount, empty data, bridge error).\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Spec alignment` — phase-by-phase coverage matrix for spec §10: one row per required operator view (eleven views), evidence column citing the file/function that delivers the view, status PASS / PASS-WITH-CAVEATS / BLOCK.\n- `## Coding-standard alignment` — line-by-line audit vs. `coding.md` hard limits: file lengths (`wc -l` table for every Phase 10 file), function-length grep, forbidden-token grep (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, unjustified suppressions), error-handling audit (every mutation `try/catch` confirmed), single-source-of-truth audit (status-badge mapping in one place, i18n catalog as single source).\n- `## UI-specification compliance` — walk every section of `ui-specification.md` that applies to the review UI in order. Each subsection states the rule, lists the artifacts checked, and the verdict:\n  - §3 Accessibility baseline — focus management, keyboard, ARIA, target size, focus rings, live regions on status badges.\n  - §6 Lists & tables — pagination present on every list; sortable headers via `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents; column standards.\n  - §7 Modals & dialogs — every modal native `<dialog>`; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`; typed-confirm on high-scope destructive actions in `ProposalDialog`.\n  - §8 State management — loading, empty, error states on every surface; every mutation has `try/catch` + `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`; error message style obeys Nielsen #9.\n  - §11 Visual standards — status badges use the single shared component; semantic color tokens, not hard-coded colors.\n  - §12 Data display — dates via `Intl.DateTimeFormat`; truncation on long content.\n  - §14 Internationalization — all user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)`; no inline literals; ICU placeholders on interpolated strings; no string concatenation for user text.\n  - §16 Telemetry-friendly markup — `data-testid` on every interactive element; format `data-testid=\"review-<view>-<action>\"`.\n- `## i18n coverage` — table of every user-facing string the review UI emits, with the i18n key and the namespace; flag any inline literal that escaped catalog wrapping as a blocker.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `grep -q '## Spec alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms spec alignment is a dedicated section, not prose.\n- `grep -q '## UI-specification compliance' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms UI-spec compliance walked explicitly.\n- `grep -q '## i18n coverage' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms i18n coverage table present.\n- `grep -q '## Verdict' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review concludes with a binding verdict.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Coding-standard alignment` and `## Findings` headings are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"92e01477-56ca-4f53-90e6-aa05679be5ea","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2082:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2738:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:788:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013zgGbBpL5RCG9X6xGX3GFk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LLzhtrw7NNpnBWtrst5QqD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T36.md","content":"# T36 — Phase 5 — MCP surface for working memory inspection + event capture for include/exclude reasons\n\n**Phase:** 5\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T34, T35\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [§5. Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#5-working-memory), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log), [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the MCP tool that lets a client inspect the current `WorkingMemoryState` and the event-capture hooks that automatically log included and excluded context per spec §Phase 5 deliverable (\"automatic event capture for included and excluded context\"). The MCP tool exposes the same compact-by-default shape the rest of the MCP surface uses; the event hooks emit `MemoryRetrieved` and `MemoryExpanded` events (Phase 2 event kinds) every time the working memory's `selected_memories` or `excluded_memories` change. Together these make working memory observable — meeting the Phase 5 Definition of Done (\"workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\").\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: ... MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context ...\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Required event types: ... ContextBundleReturned; MemoryRetrieved; MemoryExpanded ...\" (spec §3. Event Log)\n\n> \"Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Every tool response should support: compact rendering; full structured JSON; context handles; stable expansion targets; budget controls.\" (spec §9. MCP Surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T34 `WorkingMemoryState` and checkpoint API.\n  - T35 `OpContext`, eight operations, and `record_state_mutation` internal hook.\n  - Phase 2 event-log API — exposes `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `ContextBundleReturned` writers.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server registration.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` dispatch and tool-schema declarations.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` — context-handle convention for round-trippable handles.\n  - Phase 2 event writer (assumed to live at `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/`).\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, single source of truth — reuse the existing MCP dispatch convention); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (compact-by-default; build/deploy procedure for the daemon).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Phase 5 deliverables, §3. Event Log event-kind list, and §9. MCP Surface response contract to fix the tool shape and event-emission contract.\n2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`: register a new MCP tool `inspect_working_memory` with schema `{ task_id: string, mode: \"compact\" | \"diagnostic\", include_excluded: boolean }`. The handler loads the active `WorkingMemoryState` for the task (from the session-scoped in-memory store maintained by T35's `OpContext`, falling back to the latest checkpoint if the session has rolled), then returns either a compact summary (via T35 `summarize`) or the full state including `excluded_memories` when `include_excluded=true`. Response carries an `expansion_handle` per spec §9 so the caller can expand the full state without re-fetching.\n3. Wire the tool into `mcp.rs` dispatch alongside the existing 43 tools. Tool registration includes the schema in the MCP `tools/list` response; failure to expose a parameter is a contract violation caught by the cross-layer MCP schema test gate.\n4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs`: a small module exposing `emit_memory_retrieved(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)` and `emit_memory_expanded(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)`. Each function computes the diff (which identities were added to `selected_memories`, which were added to `excluded_memories` with reasons), packages the diff into an event payload per Phase 2's event-payload contract (workspace, branch, session, task, actor, timestamp, payload hash, compact summary, optional full payload location), and writes through the Phase 2 event writer.\n5. Attach the hooks to T35's `record_state_mutation` so every state-mutating operation emits the appropriate event automatically: `retrieve` and `compress` emit `MemoryRetrieved` (since they decide what is included/excluded); `expand` emits `MemoryExpanded`; `pin`, `evict`, `filter`, `checkpoint` emit `MemoryRetrieved` when they change inclusion/exclusion. `summarize` emits no event.\n6. Honor the spec invariant \"the full event stream should not be injected into prompts\" — the event hooks never put event data back into `WorkingMemoryState`. Events flow one way: state mutation → event log.\n7. Hot-path discipline: event writes share the existing Phase 2 write budget (`≤5ms P99`). If the writer returns a backpressure error, the operation does not fail — the event is dropped with a `warn!` log naming the task and operation, per `coding.md` \"no silent .catch\" but tolerant of event-log overload.\n8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` covering: tool registration appears in `tools/list`; compact mode returns a summary; diagnostic mode returns the full state and embedded `RetrievalBundle`-shaped `selected_memories`; `include_excluded=true` returns excluded entries with reasons; expansion handle round-trips back to the same state; a synthetic event writer captures one `MemoryRetrieved` event per `retrieve` call and one `MemoryExpanded` event per `expand` call; event payload carries the included and excluded identity diffs and reasons.\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` (release build verifies the whole daemon still compiles with the new tool registered), then `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::working_memory_tool_tests`. Deploy the new daemon binary per the project rule (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`) only when explicitly told to ship; the test commands themselves do not require a redeploy.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs` — MCP tool registration, schema, handler, dispatch wiring.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs` — `emit_memory_retrieved`, `emit_memory_expanded`, state-diff computation, event payload assembly.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for tool registration, compact/diagnostic modes, exclude visibility, expansion handle, event capture.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release`\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::working_memory_tool_tests`\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. The MCP handler decomposes mode handling into helpers; event-diff computation is its own function.\n- Schema parity: the MCP tool JSON schema mirrors the Rust handler's parameter struct exactly; the response shape mirrors `WorkingMemoryState` (compact view exposes a documented subset). Schema declaration and Rust struct are updated in the same commit.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: state loading uses T34's `load_checkpoint` / T35's `OpContext` session store; no parallel state lookup. Event writing uses the Phase 2 event writer; no parallel event payload assembler.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed; the backpressure-drop path logs at `warn!` with task and operation context, never silent.\n- Doc citation: tool comments cite `## 9. MCP Surface`; event-hook comments cite `## 3. Event Log` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: tool registration, compact/diagnostic modes, exclude visibility, expansion handle, automatic event capture all delivered in this task.\n- [ ] Every Phase-5 state mutation emits an event without manual call sites — the `record_state_mutation` hook is the single attachment point.\n- [ ] Backpressure on the event log degrades to a logged drop, not a failed operation.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3458,"cache_read_input_tokens":74783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3458,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"4430d74f-dcef-44ba-9df8-04d1b33b60f6","request_id":"req_011Cb7NHj1kpRYTTxArY2nhh","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2083:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2739:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:797:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DeEj2PHYWT2tYJ3BkufH5S","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R79.md","content":"# R79 — Contract gate — Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI MCP plumbing\n\n**Phase:** Contract gate after Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R64, R78\n**Spec anchor:** [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface), [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§MCP Tool Contract Principles](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-tool-contract-principles)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review is the contract gate between the Phase 10 review UI and the daemon MCP surface that powers it. Every method on `ReviewRpcBridge` calls a tool registered on the daemon side; this gate verifies that every method has a matching tool, that the JSON shapes round-trip end to end, that the schema is documented and regression-tested, and that no surface added by Phases 6–9 was forgotten on the extension side. The gate exists because UI compile + lint cannot detect contract drift — only an integration test that sends a real request to a real daemon and asserts the response shape can. Spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties demands \"every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested\"; this review enforces that for the review UI surface.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77 (extension review surface).\n- Every artifact produced by Phase 6–9 MCP work the review UI depends on, including the R64 contract gate output for the MCP tool schema.\n- Existing files this review cross-checks:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — registered MCP tools.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed extension-side bridge.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — underlying JSON-RPC client.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (from T02) — backward-compatibility policy this gate enforces.\n- Spec sections: `## 9. MCP Surface`, `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (schema parity, single source of truth); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` (backward-compatibility discipline).\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Enumerate every method exposed by `ReviewRpcBridge` and confirm each maps to a registered MCP tool in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Any orphan method on either side is a blocker.\n2. For every mapped pair, inspect the typed request and response shapes on the TypeScript side (`rpcBridge.ts`) and the corresponding Rust schema in `mcp.rs` (and its serde structs). Field-by-field comparison: type, required/optional, name. Drift is a blocker per `coding.md` §Schema parity.\n3. Cross-check `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (T02) — every review-UI tool is either `stable`, `additive`, or `redesigned-with-shim`; nothing is undocumented.\n4. Create `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` (this is the deliverable contract test). The test launches a real daemon process via the existing pattern in `extension/src/test/`, sends one round-trip request per review-UI bridge method, and asserts the response parses into the typed shape. Include happy-path requests and an error-path request per tool. Tests use the real daemon binary (`daemon/target/release/lattice`), not a mock.\n5. Run the contract suite with `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm test`. Confirm every round-trip passes. If the daemon binary is not present in `extension/bin/`, the test setup builds and copies per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy: `cd daemon && cargo build --release && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/`.\n6. Confirm the contract test exercises every review-UI tool from `rpcBridge.ts`: memory listing, evidence inspection, promotion queue read + apply + reject, contradiction queue read + apply + reject, stale listing, verify_memory, event trace read, retrieval explanation read, consolidation queue read + retry, indexing health read, graph health read + recompute.\n7. Grep `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` for the corresponding tool registrations and confirm each accepts the documented request shape and returns the documented response shape.\n8. Cross-check that backward-compat shims from R64 (if any) are still in place: every legacy tool name continues to route to the new implementation.\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Contract surfaces` — table of every `ReviewRpcBridge` method, the corresponding daemon tool name, the request shape, the response shape, and the spec citation. One row per method.\n- `## Round-trip evidence` — for every method, the contract-test name in `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` that exercises it; the test output snippet showing the request, the response, and the assertion that the typed shape parsed; the daemon log line showing the request was received.\n- `## Schema surfaces` — TypeScript ↔ Rust field comparison table per tool. Columns: TS field, TS type, TS required, Rust field, Rust type, Rust required, parity verdict. Any drift is flagged.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `test -f extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` — confirms the new contract test file exists.\n- `grep -q '## Contract surfaces' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms contract surfaces enumerated under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Round-trip evidence' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms round-trip evidence is present.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile (including the new contract test) succeeds.\n- `cd extension && npm test` — the contract suite plus all prior tests pass against a real daemon.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` exists, compiles, and passes against the real daemon binary.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Schema surfaces` and `## Findings` are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"ce6e8520-9b5f-42b8-a09b-8be0a2ce22df","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2087:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2744:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:827:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 12 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**Files written: 12**\n\n**First 3 paths confirmed:**\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md` (intent classifier)\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md` (anchor extractor + resolver)\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T29.md` (hybrid candidate retrieval)\n\n**All 12 files:** T27, T28, T29, T30, T31, T32, R33, T34, T35, T36, T37, R38.\n\n**Coverage summary:**\n- **Phase 4 (T27–T32, R33):** Intent classifier with feature-based ranker per §Ranking Complexity; anchor extractor for all 6 spec-named types (paths/symbols/errors/commands/APIs/config keys) routing through Phase 1 IdentityResolver; hybrid candidate retrieval covering all 9 spec-named sources with budget enforcement; scorer implementing every one of the 13 spec-named ranking signals with diagnostic mode; compact shaper with non-optional `inclusion_reason` and `expansion_handle` plus Phase 5 forward-compatibility contract; benchmark + golden anchors + ranking regression tests writing baseline JSON; review with required headings `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Inclusion-reason discipline`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- **Phase 5 (T34–T37, R38):** `WorkingMemoryState` carrying all 11 spec-named fields plus typed `ExcludedMemory.exclusion_reason`; `working_memory_checkpoints` table with versioned schema and SHA-256 hash; all 8 spec-named operations (retrieve/summarize/filter/pin/evict/expand/compress/checkpoint) with idempotency and pin-protection contracts; MCP `inspect_working_memory` tool plus automatic event capture wiring `MemoryRetrieved`/`MemoryExpanded` events from Phase 2; 4 test families (budgets/pins/eviction/checkpoint restore); review with required headings `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Excluded-context auditability`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n\nEvery brief uses the T01 template (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks, Definition of done), quotes the exact spec headings verbatim, lists 7–10 concrete steps, names the exact file paths from `tasks.json` `files_expected`, copies the exact `verification_commands` from `tasks.json`, and includes the requested coding-standard items (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, schema parity for the SQL task T34, no broken windows). No tasks.json, context.md, plan.md, or implementation code was modified."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a8b9cf908dfeed8f7 (use SendMessage with to: 'a8b9cf908dfeed8f7' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 90189\ntool_uses: 30\nduration_ms: 647944</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"dfd23169-64c2-432e-8b9c-fc94260da89e","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:42:55.119Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are writing markdown task brief files for a build harness. Do NOT implement the feature itself — just write the brief markdown files.\n\nRepo root: /home/pete/cadres/lattice\nSpec: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md  (READ Phases 4 and 5 sections + §Retrieval Engine + §Working Memory)\nContext: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md\nTasks JSON: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks.json\nShared standards: /home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md\n\nWrite these 12 files at `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/<TASK_ID>.md`:\n\nT27, T28, T29, T30, T31, T32, R33, T34, T35, T36, T37, R38\n\n**Use the same template structure as the prior agents** (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks, Definition of done) for work tasks; for review tasks use the review template with required headings.\n\n**Task-specific guidance:**\n\n- T27 — intent classifier: classify user task by type (debug/refactor/explain/add-feature/test/docs/migration/etc.). Should be feature-based and inspectable per spec §Ranking Complexity risk.\n- T28 — anchor extractor: extract paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys; resolve into graph identities using identity resolver from Phase 1.\n- T29 — hybrid candidate retrieval: exact path/symbol lookup + code graph traversal + doc backlinks/outgoing links + FTS + embeddings + event similarity + memory links + workflow similarity + recent working memory.\n- T30 — scoring with ALL signals from spec §Retrieval Engine \"Ranking signals\": task-type compatibility, graph proximity, exact identifier match, semantic similarity, verification status, freshness, scope, evidence strength, contradiction/supersession state, past usefulness, recent successful reuse, user preference compatibility, token cost. Diagnostic mode exposes scores AND reasons.\n- T31 — compact response shaper: every result has an `inclusion_reason` and an `expansion_handle`. Forward-compatible with Phase 5 working memory checkpoints per spec §Phase 4 scope constraint.\n- T32 — benchmark suite + golden anchors + ranking regression tests.\n- R33 — Phase 4 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Inclusion-reason discipline`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- T34 — working memory state from spec §Working Memory: task statement, interpreted intent, active files/symbols, active hypotheses, active failures, current plan, selected memories, excluded memories and reasons, budget decisions, unresolved questions, verification status. Table: `working_memory_checkpoints`.\n- T35 — operations: retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, checkpoint.\n- T36 — MCP tool for inspection + event capture for include/exclude reasons (`MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded` events from Phase 2).\n- T37 — tests: budgets, pinned context, eviction, checkpoint restore.\n- R38 — Phase 5 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Excluded-context auditability`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n\nRead the spec yourself for exact section text and quote it. Cite spec headings precisely.\n\nFor each task, include 5-10 concrete steps, exact file paths (from tasks.json `files_expected`), and the exact verification commands from tasks.json. The \"Coding-standard checks\" section should include: file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, schema parity for SQL tasks, no broken windows.\n\nWrite all 12 files, then report file count + first 3 paths confirmed. Do NOT modify tasks.json, context.md, or plan.md. Do NOT implement any code.","agentId":"a8b9cf908dfeed8f7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 12 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**Files written: 12**\n\n**First 3 paths confirmed:**\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md` (intent classifier)\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md` (anchor extractor + resolver)\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T29.md` (hybrid candidate retrieval)\n\n**All 12 files:** T27, T28, T29, T30, T31, T32, R33, T34, T35, T36, T37, R38.\n\n**Coverage summary:**\n- **Phase 4 (T27–T32, R33):** Intent classifier with feature-based ranker per §Ranking Complexity; anchor extractor for all 6 spec-named types (paths/symbols/errors/commands/APIs/config keys) routing through Phase 1 IdentityResolver; hybrid candidate retrieval covering all 9 spec-named sources with budget enforcement; scorer implementing every one of the 13 spec-named ranking signals with diagnostic mode; compact shaper with non-optional `inclusion_reason` and `expansion_handle` plus Phase 5 forward-compatibility contract; benchmark + golden anchors + ranking regression tests writing baseline JSON; review with required headings `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Inclusion-reason discipline`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- **Phase 5 (T34–T37, R38):** `WorkingMemoryState` carrying all 11 spec-named fields plus typed `ExcludedMemory.exclusion_reason`; `working_memory_checkpoints` table with versioned schema and SHA-256 hash; all 8 spec-named operations (retrieve/summarize/filter/pin/evict/expand/compress/checkpoint) with idempotency and pin-protection contracts; MCP `inspect_working_memory` tool plus automatic event capture wiring `MemoryRetrieved`/`MemoryExpanded` events from Phase 2; 4 test families (budgets/pins/eviction/checkpoint restore); review with required headings `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Excluded-context auditability`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n\nEvery brief uses the T01 template (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks, Definition of done), quotes the exact spec headings verbatim, lists 7–10 concrete steps, names the exact file paths from `tasks.json` `files_expected`, copies the exact `verification_commands` from `tasks.json`, and includes the requested coding-standard items (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, schema parity for the SQL task T34, no broken windows). No tasks.json, context.md, plan.md, or implementation code was modified."}],"totalDurationMs":647944,"totalTokens":90189,"totalToolUseCount":30,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":416,"cache_read_input_tokens":88692,"output_tokens":1080,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":416},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1080,"cache_read_input_tokens":88692,"cache_creation_input_tokens":416,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":416,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":7,"editFileCount":12,"linesAdded":938,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2089:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2746:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:841:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0158fayGk8TdCJvKN5fwBobH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QuEbpAPeDTAh2At2PyM8AZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T67.md","content":"# T67 — Phase 9 — get_memory_metrics + retrieval relevance surfaces wired through MCP\n\n**Phase:** 9\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T65\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-9-metrics-and-evaluation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task wires the T65 metrics module through the MCP surface so the `get_memory_metrics` tool from T60 returns the full canonical signal set rather than the fallback session_metrics view, and adds per-call retrieval relevance surfaces (inclusion reasons, scoring breakdowns) into the existing workflow tool responses. Spec §Phase 9 Deliverables names \"retrieval relevance metrics\" as binding, and spec §Phase 4 Definition of Done requires \"workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles … irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed.\" Without this task, the metrics module ships but no MCP client can read it and no per-call relevance signal is exposed.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 9 Deliverables: retrieval relevance metrics; memory usefulness metrics; context token efficiency metrics; stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics.\"\n\n> \"Workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles … irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed.\" (spec §Phase 4 DoD)\n\n> \"The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\" (spec §Retrieval Engine)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T65 — `MetricsCollector`, `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`, `MetricScope`.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/get_memory_metrics.rs` (T60) — current implementation reading from fallback session_metrics; T67 swaps it to consume `MetricsCollector` directly.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/` (T56/T57) — each tool's response carries `MemoryHighlight`s; T67 extends them with per-call relevance scoring.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` — for handle-driven expansion of relevance details.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits`, `## Single source of truth`, `## No broken windows`, `## Schema / model parity`, `## Observability`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 9, §Retrieval Engine, and §Phase 4 DoD in full.\n2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/metrics_surface.rs` as the MCP-facing facade over the T65 `MetricsCollector`. It exposes: (a) `MetricsSurface::collect(scope, signals)` calling the T65 collector and returning `Vec<MetricValue>`; (b) `MetricsSurface::collect_for_call(task_id, tool_name)` returning per-call relevance: per-pivot inclusion score breakdown (graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, evidence strength, past usefulness — per spec §Retrieval Engine \"Ranking signals\"), per-memory inclusion reason, excluded high-scoring candidates with rejection reasons; (c) `MetricsSurface::summarize_for_compact_mode(...)` returning a one-line digest suitable for compact tool responses.\n3. Wire `metrics_surface.rs` into `memory_v2/get_memory_metrics.rs` so the tool reads from the T65 collector through this facade rather than the fallback `session_metrics`. The fallback path remains as an explicit `source: SessionMetrics` value when the collector returns `None` for a given signal — never fabricate a value.\n4. Extend the T56/T57 workflow tools' responses to attach per-call relevance via `MetricsSurface::collect_for_call`. In `compact` render mode each `MemoryHighlight` and `Pivot` carries the summarized one-line digest; in `full` / `diagnostic` mode each carries the full score breakdown. Spec §Retrieval Engine: \"The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode.\"\n5. Add a stable expansion handle (`relevance_detail_handle`) on every `MemoryHighlight` and `Pivot` so `expand_context` can fetch the full diagnostic breakdown without re-running the workflow.\n6. Workspace boundary + scope-leak protections: every `MetricsSurface::collect_for_call` query honors workspace and scope filters, matching the discipline established in T58–T60.\n7. In `metrics_surface_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip the surface's request/response through serde; (b) assert the surface returns every T65 `MetricSignal` variant; (c) assert it returns `null` with a reason when no data is available, never a fabricated value; (d) assert per-call relevance includes every ranking-signal column from spec §Retrieval Engine; (e) assert compact mode digests are bounded in size; (f) assert the diagnostic expansion handle dereferences to the full breakdown; (g) assert workspace boundary and scope filters reject cross-scope queries with a typed error.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/metrics_surface.rs` — MCP-facing facade exposing `collect`, `collect_for_call`, `summarize_for_compact_mode`; wires T65 `MetricsCollector` into `get_memory_metrics` (T60) and extends T56/T57 workflow responses with per-call relevance breakdowns and stable expansion handles per spec §Retrieval Engine.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/metrics_surface_tests.rs` — unit tests covering serde round-trip, full signal-set coverage, honest null reporting, per-call relevance breakdown, compact-mode digest bound, expansion-handle dereference, scope-leak rejection.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/metrics_surface.rs` — confirms the surface file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::metrics_surface_tests` — every metrics-surface test passes (serde round-trip, signal coverage, honest null, per-call breakdown, compact digest bound, expansion-handle dereference, scope-leak rejection).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `MetricsSurface` request/response types reuse `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`, `MetricSource`, `MetricScope` from T65; do not introduce parallel types; every public field has a doc comment.\n- No silent error handling — missing data is honest `None` with reason; scope-leak rejections are typed errors.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`MetricsSurface::collect_for_call`, not `MetricsSurface::call_metrics`).\n- For SQLite: storage access flows through T65 collector; no raw SQL in the RPC surface.\n- Observability: surface emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `scope`, `signal_count`, `source_breakdown` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation`, `## Retrieval Engine`, `## Phase 4 — Retrieval V1` DoD in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every T65 signal is reachable via `get_memory_metrics`; every T56/T57 workflow response carries per-call relevance + expansion handle in this task — not \"to be added later.\"\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the legacy `session_metrics` view continues to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3897,"cache_read_input_tokens":99814,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3897,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"a198de81-caba-4d7a-a742-bf4416702711","request_id":"req_011Cb7NUqgQADaeKzWbKLxf2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
